export const linuxProjectGuides: Record<string, string> = {
	"LS5-Systemd-Toy-Service":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the guide's literal shell variables.
		'# LS5 Systemd Toy Service\n\nManage a small long-lived process with a real systemd user manager. This required\nproject belongs to Services and systemd. The preceding shell exercises establish\ncommand, output and exit-status habits; this project adds manager state, process\nidentity, journaling, restart policy and persistent enablement.\n\n## Environment and learner contract\n\nUse an owned Debian/Ubuntu Linux VM or systemd-enabled WSL distribution with\nPython 3 and a working user manager. Check `uname -s`, `id`,\n`ps -p 1 -o comm=`, `systemctl --version` and\n`systemctl --user show --property=Version --value`.\nCheck that `journalctl --user` can read this user\'s service journal. A manager\nconnection or journal-access failure is an unmet prerequisite to resolve in\nthe lab before claiming service completion. A parsed unit file or direct\nPython run covers different evidence.\n\nBoth roles use the same driver and user unit. Complete only the learner\n`render_heartbeat(message, number)` function: return\n`<message>: heartbeat <number>`. The untouched function returns None, emits\na diagnostic and exits 78 before any heartbeat. The unit prevents automatic\nrestart for that unfinished-task status. Compare the reference after an\nindependent completion.\n\nHEARTBEAT_INTERVAL is a decimal from 0.1 through 60 seconds, without signs,\nexponents or padded integer parts. HEARTBEAT_MESSAGE is one nonempty UTF-8\nline of at most 128 bytes. HEARTBEAT_LIMIT is 0 for continuous operation or\na canonical integer from 1 through 9999. Invalid settings exit 2 before\nproducing a heartbeat. Each valid row is flushed immediately. A finite run\nstops after its requested row count; a service uses continuous operation.\n\n## Direct prediction and controlled input\n\nFrom the selected role folder, run:\n\n```bash\nHEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=0.1 HEARTBEAT_MESSAGE=sample HEARTBEAT_LIMIT=2 python3 heartbeat_app.py\nHEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=0 python3 heartbeat_app.py\nHEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=nan python3 heartbeat_app.py\n```\n\nThe reference and independently completed learner print exactly:\n\n```text\nsample: heartbeat 1\nsample: heartbeat 2\n```\n\nThe valid untouched learner exits 78 with its named unfinished diagnostic.\nThe two invalid settings exit 2 in both roles. Record stdout, stderr, status,\nelapsed time and the different reasons. Add a valid boundary and a Unicode\nmessage case. These checks establish the app contract before manager operation.\n\n## Install a unique owned user unit\n\nKeep this shell open. The guarded setup refuses to replace an\nexisting install or unit. It reads HOME without changing its value.\n\n```bash\nrole_dir=$(pwd -P)\nlab_name="linux-heartbeat-$(id -u)-$$"\nlab_unit="$lab_name.service"\ninstall_root="$HOME/.local/share/$lab_name"\nunit_root="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user"\nif test ! -e "$install_root" &&\\\n   test ! -e "$unit_root/$lab_unit"; then\nmkdir -p "$install_root" "$unit_root"\ncp "$role_dir/heartbeat_app.py" "$install_root/heartbeat_app.py"\ncp "$role_dir/heartbeat.env.example" "$install_root/heartbeat.env"\npython3 - "$role_dir/linux-heartbeat.service" "$unit_root/$lab_unit" "$lab_name" <<\'PY\'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nsource, target, name = sys.argv[1:]\ntext = Path(source).read_text()\nprefix = "%h/.local/share/linux-heartbeat"\nassert text.count(prefix) == 3\nPath(target).write_text(text.replace(prefix, "%h/.local/share/" + name))\nPY\nsystemd-analyze --user verify "$unit_root/$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user cat "$lab_unit"\nelse\n  printf \'The lab name is already in use. Stop here and choose a new name.\\n\' >&2\nfi\n```\n\nThe unit is a user unit: %h identifies the user\'s home; the process inherits\nthe user manager\'s identity. It needs no www-data account or /opt installation.\nWorkingDirectory, the required environment file and ExecStart point into the\nunique installed directory. The supplied Python binary is /usr/bin/python3;\nverify that path in this Debian/Ubuntu lab.\n\nThe environment file sets the interval to 0.25 seconds and a distinct message.\nEnvironmentFile values override the defaults supplied by Environment. Changing\nits contents requires an app restart; changing unit directives also requires\ndaemon-reload before restart. Neither operation is a substitute for observed\nrunning state and output.\n\n## Start, inspect, enable and stop\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --user start "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user status "$lab_unit" --no-pager\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=ActiveState,SubState,MainPID,ExecMainStatus,Result,NRestarts,InvocationID\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 20\n```\n\nType=simple may report a successful start command before the app later fails.\nThe untouched starter program must settle in failed state with ExecMainStatus=78,\nMainPID=0 and no restart loop. Complete the named function in the learner source\nthen copy the changed app to the installed path:\n\n```bash\ncp "$role_dir/heartbeat_app.py" "$install_root/heartbeat_app.py"\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user start "$lab_unit"\n```\n\nFor a completed app, require active/running state, a nonzero MainPID and actual\njournal rows from that process. Inspect only the recorded owned PID:\n\n```bash\nmain_pid=$(systemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=MainPID --value)\nps -p "$main_pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,args\nsystemctl --user enable "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user is-enabled "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user stop "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=ActiveState,SubState,MainPID\n```\n\nEnable creates the persistent user-target activation link; it does not itself\nstart the app. A stopped unit is inactive with MainPID=0 even while enabled.\nRecord the actual target link and explain this distinction. Start the unit\nagain for the following experiments with `systemctl --user start "$lab_unit"`.\n\n## Manual restart and unexpected failure\n\nRecord the current MainPID and invocation, restart with\n`systemctl --user restart "$lab_unit"`, and observe a new PID and new journal\nrows. Each app process restarts its heartbeat numbering at 1.\n\nFor an automatic-restart case, first verify the current PID belongs to the\ninstalled heartbeat app and matches the unit\'s MainPID. Send SIGKILL only to\nthat recorded process:\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"\nmain_pid=$(systemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=MainPID --value)\ntest "$main_pid" -gt 0\nps -p "$main_pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,args\nkill -KILL "$main_pid"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=ActiveState,MainPID,NRestarts,Result\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 30\n```\n\nResetting the failed state before this isolated case also resets its start-limit\ncounters. Record a fresh restart-count baseline after that reset.\n\nWait for the restart delay, then require a new running PID, NRestarts greater\nthan its earlier value and new heartbeat rows. An explicit stop is different:\nit requests termination and does not trigger on-failure restart.\n\n## Configuration failure, recovery and undo\n\nSave the working environment file, then replace it with an invalid interval:\n\n```bash\ncp "$install_root/heartbeat.env" "$install_root/heartbeat-good.env"\nprintf \'HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=0\\nHEARTBEAT_MESSAGE=broken-lab\\nHEARTBEAT_LIMIT=0\\n\' >"$install_root/heartbeat.env"\nsystemctl --user restart "$lab_unit"\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 30\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=ActiveState,SubState,ExecMainStatus,Result,NRestarts\n```\n\nThe app exits 2. The bounded restart policy eventually produces a failed unit\nafter the start limit. Record the actual diagnostic, restart count and manager\nresult; do not infer success from the restart command\'s return alone.\n\nRestore the good environment, use `systemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"`\nand start it again. Confirm active/running state, a new process with the owned\nidentity and fresh journal rows bearing the restored message.\n\nComplete service-notes.md with the environment, versions, source role, direct\napp cases, installed paths, real manager states, PID/UID evidence, journal\nrows, enable/stop distinction, manual and automatic restarts, failure,\nrecovery and cleanup. The reference explains predicted behavior; the record\nrequires actual observations. A walkthrough can pause at each prediction,\nstate transition and repair; the same sequence supports independent reading.\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --user stop "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user disable "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"\nrm "$unit_root/$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=LoadState,ActiveState,MainPID\n```\n\nRecord the disappearance of the owned process and activation link. Keep the\nsmall installed source/evidence directory for the completion record. Remove it\nlater only after confirming it is the unique directory created above.\n\nReferences: [systemd.service](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.service.html),\n[systemctl](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemctl.html),\n[systemd.unit](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.unit.html).\n',
	"LS4-Apache-Virtual-Host-Compare":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve literal Apache environment placeholders.
		'# LS4 Apache Virtual Host Compare\n\nServe the same small site with Apache and Nginx, then explain how each server\nselects a virtual host and document root. This required project follows the\nstatic Nginx lab. The reverse-proxy app is a separate project: these servers\nserve files directly.\n\n## Environment and source roles\n\nUse an unprivileged account in an owned Debian/Ubuntu Linux VM or WSL\ndistribution. Apache 2.4, Nginx, Python 3, curl and ss must already be available.\nFor Debian/Ubuntu, the Apache binary is `apache2` and its modules are under\n`/usr/lib/apache2/modules`. Check `apache2 -v`, `apache2 -l`, `nginx -v`,\n`uname -s` and `id` first. These commands use explicit owned configurations\nand loopback ports; the lab needs no public hostname, certificate or system\nservice changes.\n\nBoth roles include an Apache configuration, a complete comparison Nginx\nconfiguration and two UTF-8 pages. The reference Apache primary virtual host\npoints at `public`. The learner Apache primary host deliberately points at\nthe empty `unfinished` folder. Its one named `document_root` TODO is changing\nthat primary `DocumentRoot` to the supplied `public` folder. Preserve the\nsecond host, access rules, listeners, logs and Nginx configuration.\n\nThe untouched learner\'s request for primary `/index.html` returns 404; the\nNginx comparison already returns the supplied page. The learner\'s second Apache\nhost works. Predict these differences before completing the task. Compare\n`solution` after an independent attempt.\n\n## Prepare a reproducible owned prefix\n\nFrom the selected `starter` or `solution` folder, run commands individually.\nKeep this shell open so its environment and owned process identities remain\navailable. If either chosen port is occupied, choose unused ports above 1023\nand update both Nginx `listen` lines in the installed copy.\n\n```bash\nrole_dir=$(pwd -P)\nlab_root=$(mktemp -d)\nmkdir -p "$lab_root"/{run,logs,public,second,unfinished}\ncp "$role_dir/index.html" "$lab_root/public/index.html"\ncp "$role_dir/second.html" "$lab_root/second/index.html"\ncp "$role_dir/linux-systems-apache.conf" "$lab_root/apache.conf"\ncp "$role_dir/linux-systems-nginx.conf" "$lab_root/nginx.conf"\nexport LAB_ROOT="$lab_root" APACHE_PORT=18080\nexport LAB_UID="$(id -u)" LAB_GID="$(id -g)"\nss -ltn \'( sport = :18080 or sport = :18081 )\'\napache2 -f "$lab_root/apache.conf" -t\napache2 -f "$lab_root/apache.conf" -S\nnginx -p "$lab_root/" -c nginx.conf -t\nnginx -p "$lab_root/" -c nginx.conf -T\n```\n\nThe Apache configuration expands these explicit environment values. Its\n`ServerRoot`, runtime directory, PID file and logs stay in this prefix.\nNginx resolves its relative roots, logs and PID file through `-p`. Apache\nuses the current numeric user and group; Nginx inherits the invoking identity.\nRecord the actual versions, UID/GID, chosen ports, syntax results and two host\nmappings. Syntax success is a separate checkpoint from serving.\n\n## Start, request and inspect\n\nRegister cleanup before starting either process. The following function only\naddresses the two recorded background processes and waits for each separately.\nKeep the temporary prefix for inspecting evidence after stopping.\n\n```bash\napache_pid=""\nnginx_pid=""\ncleanup_lab() {\n    if [ -n "$apache_pid" ]; then\n        kill -TERM "$apache_pid" 2>/dev/null\n        wait "$apache_pid"\n        apache_pid=""\n    fi\n    if [ -n "$nginx_pid" ]; then\n        kill -TERM "$nginx_pid" 2>/dev/null\n        wait "$nginx_pid"\n        nginx_pid=""\n    fi\n}\ntrap cleanup_lab EXIT INT TERM\napache2 -f "$lab_root/apache.conf" -DFOREGROUND >"$lab_root/apache-stdout.log" 2>&1 &\napache_pid=$!\nnginx -p "$lab_root/" -c nginx.conf -g \'daemon off;\' >"$lab_root/nginx-stdout.log" 2>&1 &\nnginx_pid=$!\n```\n\nUse a new request after the listeners appear. For chosen alternative ports,\nreplace the URLs below. Save the response body and status independently.\n\n```bash\nss -ltnp \'( sport = :18080 or sport = :18081 )\'\nps -p "$apache_pid,$nginx_pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,args\ncat "$lab_root/run/apache.pid" "$lab_root/run/nginx.pid"\ncurl -sS -H \'Host: linux-systems.test\' -o "$lab_root/apache-primary.body" -w \'%{http_code}\\n\' http://127.0.0.1:18080/index.html\ncurl -sS -H \'Host: linux-systems.test\' -o "$lab_root/nginx-primary.body" -w \'%{http_code}\\n\' http://127.0.0.1:18081/index.html\ncurl -i -H \'Host: second.test\' http://127.0.0.1:18080/index.html\ncurl -i -H \'Host: second.test\' http://127.0.0.1:18081/index.html\ncurl -I -H \'Host: linux-systems.test\' http://127.0.0.1:18080/index.html\ncurl -i -H \'Host: other.test\' http://127.0.0.1:18080/index.html\ncurl -i -H \'Host: other.test\' http://127.0.0.1:18081/index.html\ncurl -i -H \'Host: linux-systems.test\' http://127.0.0.1:18080/missing.txt\ncurl -i -H \'Host: linux-systems.test\' http://127.0.0.1:18081/missing.txt\n```\n\nThe two fictional hostnames need no hosts-file or DNS edits because curl sends\nthe Host header. Both listeners remain 127.0.0.1. The PID files must identify\nthe recorded parent processes. The secondary page is distinct. Unknown hosts\nfall back to the first configured host in these configurations; that routing\nchoice supplies no authentication. Missing files return 404. A successful\nHEAD has the UTF-8 file\'s byte Content-Length and no response body.\n\nFor the learner, edit only the installed Apache primary `DocumentRoot` TODO,\nthen save the same correction to the learner source. Validate with `-t`,\nreload with `kill -USR1 "$apache_pid"`, and repeat the primary request until\nthe new configuration is observed. Successful primary bodies must match the\nsupplied `index.html` exactly:\n\n```bash\ncmp "$role_dir/index.html" "$lab_root/apache-primary.body"\ncmp "$role_dir/index.html" "$lab_root/nginx-primary.body"\ntail -n 10 "$lab_root/logs/apache-access.log" "$lab_root/logs/nginx-access.log"\n```\n\n## Change configuration, observe reload and undo\n\nSave the working installed configurations first:\n\n```bash\ncp "$lab_root/apache.conf" "$lab_root/apache-good.conf"\ncp "$lab_root/nginx.conf" "$lab_root/nginx-good.conf"\npython3 - "$lab_root" <<\'PY\'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nroot = Path(sys.argv[1])\nchanges = [\n    ("apache.conf", \'DocumentRoot "${LAB_ROOT}/public"\', \'DocumentRoot "${LAB_ROOT}/second"\'),\n    ("nginx.conf", "root public;", "root second;"),\n]\nfor name, before, after in changes:\n    path = root / name\n    text = path.read_text()\n    assert text.count(before) == 1\n    path.write_text(text.replace(before, after, 1))\nPY\napache2 -f "$lab_root/apache.conf" -t\nnginx -p "$lab_root/" -c nginx.conf -t\nkill -USR1 "$apache_pid"\nkill -HUP "$nginx_pid"\n```\n\nRepeat fresh primary requests for up to five seconds until both return the\nsecond page. Record the changed configuration, status and exact body. The\nsecondary host remains unchanged. Graceful reload keeps the recorded parent\nPID; stop/start creates a new identity.\n\nRestore the two good copies, validate both again, reload with the same signals\nand verify the primary page returns. For a failure case, append\n`InvalidOwnedLabDirective` to the installed Apache configuration. Its syntax\ncheck must fail. Keep that rejected configuration out of a reload; a request\nstill uses the existing working server. Restore the good copy and verify syntax.\nRepeat with `invalid_owned_lab_directive;` in the installed Nginx configuration.\nRecord each actual failure status and diagnostic.\n\n## Completion and cleanup\n\nComplete `compare-notes.md` with the two host/root mappings, explicit activation\nboundary, reload signals, UID/GID, PID/socket identities, status/body/HEAD\nobservations, rejected configuration diagnostics and restoration evidence.\nExplain why an unknown hostname follows the first host, why a copied file needs\na separate serving observation, and why a failed syntax check must not be\nfollowed by reload.\n\nIn a distribution-managed Apache site, `sites-available` stores configurations\nand `sites-enabled` selects them, commonly through `a2ensite`. Here, the explicit\n`-f` configuration is the lab\'s activation route. A separate supervised\nthrowaway-VM exercise can compare the managed-service layout after this owned\nconfiguration exercise is complete.\n\n```bash\ncleanup_lab\ntrap - EXIT INT TERM\nss -ltn \'( sport = :18080 or sport = :18081 )\'\n```\n\nBoth owned listeners must disappear. Preserve the small temporary prefix and\nsource edits with the completion record. A walkthrough can pause at prediction,\nthe learner\'s 404, correction, comparison, rejected syntax, reload and undo; the\nsame commands and record support independent reading.\n\nReferences: [Apache startup](https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/invoking.html),\n[Apache directives](https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/mod/core.html),\n[Apache graceful restart](https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/stopping.html),\n[Nginx process control](https://nginx.org/en/docs/control.html).\n',
	"LS1-Personal-Lab-VM":
		"# Linux lab identity and readiness\n\nThe report observes an existing Linux guest, WSL instance or owned remote lab.\nIt does not provision a VM, install packages, create users or change privilege\npolicy. A non-Linux host shell returns status 2 before reporting readiness.\nThe active identity comes from id, not a replaceable USER environment value.\nThe script interpreter and configured login shell are labeled separately.\n\n## Provision one owned environment before reporting readiness\n\nFor a VM, use an already approved hypervisor and a dedicated new virtual disk.\nA lab allocation of two virtual CPUs, 4 GB RAM and 32 GB disk leaves room for the\ncourse. Download the Ubuntu Server installer matching the guest architecture\nfrom Ubuntu, attach the ISO, select NAT networking and boot the new guest. Choose\nlanguage, keyboard and DHCP networking, then use only the new virtual disk for\nthe guided storage layout. Create an ordinary login account and a lab hostname,\ncomplete installation, detach the ISO and reboot. Log in, record /etc/os-release\nand uname -m, and take a named hypervisor snapshot before later changes. Shut\ndown, reopen the VM and confirm the same account and workspace are available.\nThese lab allocations are course choices, not claims about platform minimums.\nThe [Ubuntu installation guide](https://ubuntu.com/server/docs/tutorial/basic-installation/)\nsupports the installer sequence.\n\nFor Windows 11 or a supported Windows 10 build, open an administrator PowerShell\nand run wsl --list --online. Choose the listed Ubuntu distribution intended for\nthe lab, then run wsl --install -d NAME, substituting that exact listed name.\nRestart if requested, launch the distribution, and create its Linux account.\nIn PowerShell, wsl --list --verbose must show version 2; if it shows version 1,\nuse wsl --set-version NAME 2. Open the Linux distribution shell and record its\nactual release before running course commands. The\n[Microsoft WSL guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/install)\nsupports these commands. Linux paths and Windows paths remain different.\n\nAn owned remote lab is another route: connect with its assigned SSH account,\nrecord that host's identity and permitted scope, and obtain its snapshot/reset\nmethod. A shared or production host is not a disposable course VM. The report\nbelow verifies observations after provisioning; it cannot prove that the VM,\nWSL distribution or reset route was created successfully.\n\n## Run and interpret the observations\n\nExtract one role and this guide. In the Linux lab, run:\n\n~~~sh\nbash lab_bootstrap_report.sh > readiness.txt 2> diagnostics.txt\nreport_status=$?\nprintf 'status=%s\\n' \"$report_status\"\ncat readiness.txt\ncat /etc/os-release\nps -p 1 -o comm=\nid\n~~~\n\nA successful report identifies the active account, kernel, working directory,\navailable commands and major filesystem locations. In the reference, WSL is\nidentified from the Linux proc version; systemd-detect-virt can identify another\nvirtualized context. The generic linux label states only that the kernel check\npassed and a more specific context was not detected. Missing commands are\nobservations, not permission to install them on the host.\n\nThe starter's four TODO observations remain required: Linux context, course\nworkspace, package manager and privilege availability. Record distribution,\nrelease, architecture, PID 1, identity, group membership, network boundary,\nworkspace and the VM's actual snapshot/reset route. An available sudo command\ndoes not itself prove authorization for a later state change. Explain how the\nworkspace can be reopened and which later labs depend on missing tools.\n\n## Walkthrough and independent evidence\n\nTrace the early kernel guard before command inventory. Compare active identity\nwith configured shell information. Record a missing-tool case and the host-shell\nfailure separately from a healthy Linux report. The supplied course readiness\ntranscript is a clearly fictional reasoning route when a compatible Linux lab\nis unavailable; it is not an observed VM. A successful script does not establish\nthat a VM was provisioned or complete the readiness record.\n",
	"LS-05-unit-1-shell-foundations":
		"# Shell foundations: trustworthy log summary\n\nThis project composes enumeration, quoting, structured filtering, aggregation,\ndiagnostics and exit status. It reads an immediate directory of regular .log\nfiles. It does not rewrite the inputs or search subdirectories. A symbolic link\nis not a regular input in this exercise. The timestamp is field one, the level\nis field two, and the remaining text is the message. Only the exact levels ERROR\nand WARNING contribute to their respective totals.\n\n## Concepts and worked prediction\n\nThe directory contains three log files, including an empty file and a filename\nwith a space. app 1.log contains two errors and one warning; app-2.log contains\none error and one warning. An INFO message containing the word ERROR contributes\nno error. notes.txt is excluded. The reference result is therefore:\n\n~~~text\nfiles=3 errors=3 warnings=2\n~~~\n\nfind emits NUL-separated paths because a line is not a safe filename boundary.\nThe read loop uses the same separator and quotes each path. The manifest is\nwritten before aggregation so an enumeration failure retains its failure\nstatus. awk counts the level field and returns two numeric values even when a\nfile is empty. The totals are printed only after all selected inputs are read.\nDiagnostics remain on standard error. Successful empty input is different from\nan unreadable or missing directory.\n\n## Open and run\n\nUse a Linux lab with Bash, find, awk and mktemp. Extract the selected role folder\nwith its reports subdirectory, retaining this guide. From that folder:\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh\nbash task.sh reports > summary.txt 2> diagnostics.txt\ntask_status=$?\nprintf 'status=%s\\n' \"$task_status\"\ncat summary.txt\ncat diagnostics.txt\n~~~\n\nThe relative default is anchored to the script location, so running the script\nfrom another working directory selects the same supplied reports. An explicit\ndirectory argument selects a changed case. A missing directory returns status\n1 and no successful summary. An extra argument returns status 2.\n\n## Learner work and independent checks\n\nThe starter already reads and counts the inputs, but deliberately discards the\nwarning count at the named TODO. Remove that defect without changing the error\ncount, input format or output format. Record the prediction before running it.\nCreate a separate empty directory, a directory with one matching line, and a\ndirectory with multiple matching lines. Add an INFO message containing ERROR,\na non-log text file and a filename containing spaces. Record summary, diagnostic\noutput and status for each case. Test a missing directory as a failure case.\nCompare the reference only after recording a working attempt.\n\n## Instructor walkthrough and completion evidence\n\nWalk the supplied paths through find, the NUL read loop and awk before running\nthe complete pipeline. Explain why grep with an unconditional 'or true' cannot\ndistinguish no matches from an input error. Completion requires the named TODO\nrepair, the changed-case record and an explanation of the status boundary;\nrunning a supplied program does not complete that reasoning record.\n",
	"LS2-Static-Site-from-srv":
		"# Static content under /srv: publish and verify exact state\n\nThe page installer accepts an explicit document root inside the owned Linux lab.\nIt anchors source index.html to the script's directory, not the working directory.\nNo system Nginx configuration is changed by the script. The starter's named TODO\nis deliberately unfinished; the reference installs the neighboring page with\n0644 mode, applies requested ownership, then publishes it. Ownership errors are\nfatal and retain an earlier page. Ownership changes target only the document\nroot and staged page, never a recursive tree of unrelated files.\n\n## Independent filesystem run\n\nExtract one role with index.html and the configuration. In Linux:\n\n~~~sh\nlab_root=$(mktemp -d)\nbash deploy_static_site.sh \"$lab_root/public\"\nstat -c '%a %U:%G %n' \"$lab_root/public\" \"$lab_root/public/index.html\"\ncmp index.html \"$lab_root/public/index.html\"\n~~~\n\nThe root is 0755 and the page is 0644. Omitting owner preserves the active\naccount's ownership; an explicit OWNER:GROUP must be applied successfully.\nRepeat from another working directory, with spaces in the target path, and with\nan invalid owner. For the failure case, create an earlier known page and verify\nits bytes remain unchanged. Record output, diagnostics and status. Complete the\nstarter TODO without replacing its ownership/error boundary.\n\n## Owned VM Nginx route\n\nThe supplied server block listens only on 127.0.0.1:8080 and has no default_server\nclaim. This avoids conflict with the distribution's ordinary default site and\ndoes not create a public listener. In the disposable VM, publish to\n/srv/linux-systems-site, using its intended serving owner when administrative\npermission is available. Inspect the exact source and target paths before a\nprivileged command. Copy the reviewed block into the VM's sites-available folder\nand enable only its named symlink in sites-enabled. Validate with nginx -t before\nreloading the VM's Nginx service. Request http://127.0.0.1:8080/ from that VM,\ncompare the served page, and request an unknown path to verify status 404.\n\nFor a temporary document root, replace only the root directive with the exact\npublished absolute path before enabling that block. Each parent directory must\nallow the Nginx worker identity to traverse it; a default private mktemp parent\ndoes not grant that traversal. Use the owned /srv route or an explicitly planned\nlab layout rather than opening unrelated home directories recursively.\n\nRecord the account, directory modes, syntax-test result, reload result, served\nbytes and unknown-path status independently. A successful copy does not prove\nthat Nginx is serving that path. Preserve the prior VM configuration and disable\nonly the named lab site to undo it; validate before the restoration reload.\nCompletion includes the TODO, changed cases, serving observations and an undo.\n",
	"LS-06-unit-3-users-groups-and-permissions":
		'# Users, groups and permissions: diagnose ancestor traversal\n\nThis Linux project creates one bounded directory tree under an empty directory\nowned by an unprivileged account. It changes no system accounts, supplementary\ngroups or unrelated paths. Running as root is rejected because root can bypass\nthe access failure this lesson observes.\n\n## Permission model\n\nEach mode has owner, group and other classes. Read on a regular file permits\nreading its bytes; write permits modifying bytes. Directory read permits\nlisting names, execute permits traversal and access to named children, and\nwrite plus execute permits creating or removing directory entries. Deleting a\nfile is principally a permission on its containing directory, not its file\nwrite bit. Effective group membership includes supplementary groups.\n\nThe supplied file is 0640 and belongs to the active account. Its private parent\nis 0600: the same owner has read and write on the directory, but no traversal.\nThe file remains unreadable through that path. Adding owner execute to that\nparent repairs access without broadening group or other permissions.\n\n## Create, inspect and correct the owned lab\n\nExtract one role and the guide. In an unprivileged Linux shell:\n\n~~~sh\nlab_root=$(mktemp -d)\nbash task.sh setup "$lab_root"\nbash task.sh inspect "$lab_root"\nbash task.sh fix "$lab_root"\nbash task.sh verify "$lab_root"\n~~~\n\nSetup prints can_read=no. Before the correction, verify returns status 1.\nThe reference fix prints can_read=yes; final verify confirms parent mode 0700\nand unchanged file mode 0640. The starter fix is deliberately unfinished at\nthe named TODO. Replace it with one targeted command. Record the predicted and\nobserved results in permissions.md before comparing the reference.\n\nTo undo the correction inside this lab, set only the private parent back to\n0600 and repeat inspect. To remove the disposable tree, first restore owner\ntraversal with the completed fix, verify the recorded lab_root value, then\nremove exactly that directory. Setup refuses nonempty directories and later\noperations require the owned marker and nonsymlink directory layout.\n\n## Supplied service-identity case\n\nThe following case is fictional; the program\'s physical observations do not\nprove access for other accounts. Predict it from identities and complete paths:\n\n~~~text\n0750 root:signal-web /srv/signal-garden\n2750 deploy:signal-web /srv/signal-garden/releases\n0640 deploy:signal-web /srv/signal-garden/releases/index.html\ndeploy and www-data belong to signal-web; observer does not.\n~~~\n\ndeploy can traverse, read and write the file, and create or remove entries in\nreleases. www-data can traverse and read, but cannot write the file or change\ndirectory entries. observer cannot traverse the first directory. A shared\ngroup does not automatically grant write permission; the mode bits govern it.\nThe setgid bit on releases makes newly created entries inherit its group,\nsubject to the actual creation mode and umask.\n\n## Walkthrough and completion evidence\n\nExplain the owner/group/other selection before each observation. Locate the\nfirst failed ancestor instead of recursively changing permissions. Completion\nrequires the TODO repair, the independent record, the supplied identity matrix\nand an observed undo. A successful reference program does not complete the\nworksheet or establish access for the fictional service accounts.\n',
	"LS6-Backup-Cron-and-Timer":
		'# Backups, cron and a user timer\n\nThis Linux lab archives an explicit source directory into a separate owned\nbackup directory. Both scheduler examples use the same unprivileged account\nand explicit paths. The systemd files are user units, not system units: installing\nthem with a system-wide manager would select the wrong identity and home.\n\n## Archive and retention contract\n\nRun Bash with GNU find, sort, tar, mktemp, realpath and the util-linux flock command.\nSource and destination may contain spaces. The destination must be outside the\nsource tree. RETENTION_COUNT is a canonical integer from 1 through 1000; zero\nand malformed values are rejected before an archive is created. The default is\nfive. Managed archive names begin with linux-systems-backup- and end in .tar.gz;\nreserve that naming convention for this lab. Other files and symlinks are not\nselected for retention.\n\nEach successful run creates a new timestamped name with a unique suffix. A lock\nserializes runs sharing a destination. tar writes a temporary archive and only\nthen publishes it without replacing any existing archive. A failed creation\nremoves temporary files and leaves earlier backups intact. The reference keeps\nthe archive just created plus the newest remaining managed archives, up to the\nretention count. Retention failure is an error, not a success report. Archive\nmembers are relative to the source, so restoration can target a separate tree.\n\n## First run and independent restoration\n\nUse a disposable Linux workspace containing fabricated course files:\n\n~~~sh\nlab_root=$(mktemp -d)\nmkdir -p "$lab_root/source notes" "$lab_root/backup copies" "$lab_root/restored"\nprintf \'first observation\\n\' > "$lab_root/source notes/session.txt"\nbash backup_home.sh "$lab_root/source notes" "$lab_root/backup copies"\nfind "$lab_root/backup copies" -name \'linux-systems-backup-*.tar.gz\' -print\n~~~\n\nSelect the archive path printed by the program. List its members with tar -tzf,\nthen extract this known lab archive with tar -xzf into the separate restored\ndirectory. Compare session.txt against the original with cmp. Record the archive\nname, input, restored bytes, status and any diagnostics. Archive creation alone\ndoes not prove restoration.\n\nThe starter creates correct, unique archives but its named retention TODO is\nunfinished. Complete that one operation without replacing the input contract,\nlock or publication sequence. Make seven backups with changed content and\nRETENTION_COUNT=2, then verify two managed archives remain and the newest\nrestores the newest input. Repeat with retention one, an unrelated file, a\nmissing source, zero retention and paths containing spaces. Earlier backups\nremain unchanged when creation fails. Compare the reference after an attempted\nrepair and independent restore.\n\n## Install the selected role for a user scheduler\n\nIn the owned Linux lab, create the source and backup directories and install\nthe selected completed script. These commands do not configure a system service:\n\n~~~sh\nmkdir -p "$HOME/.local/bin" "$HOME/linux-systems-lab" "$HOME/backups"\ninstall -m 0755 backup_home.sh "$HOME/.local/bin/backup_home.sh"\nmkdir -p "$HOME/.config/systemd/user"\ninstall -m 0644 linux-backup.service linux-backup.timer "$HOME/.config/systemd/user/"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user start linux-backup.service\nsystemctl --user status linux-backup.service\njournalctl --user -u linux-backup.service --no-pager\nsystemctl --user enable --now linux-backup.timer\nsystemctl --user list-timers\n~~~\n\nThe %h specifier expands to the user manager\'s home. No root HOME default is\nused. A user manager may require an active login session; record actual manager\navailability and behavior rather than assuming it exists in every VM or WSL\nsession. If unavailable, use the manual run and supplied unit reasoning route.\nFor cron, inspect the existing user crontab first and add only the one example\nentry. Do not replace an existing crontab wholesale. The example sets a known\nshell and PATH and uses explicit source and destination arguments. Test one\nscheduler at a time; the destination lock also prevents overlapping archive\npublication. Verify actual scheduled output and restoration.\n\n## Undo and completion evidence\n\nDisable and stop the named user timer, then remove only the two installed lab\nunit files and run user daemon-reload. Remove only the added cron line if it was\nused. Retain earlier backups until the independent restore has been verified.\nCompletion includes the retention TODO, changed cases, an actual restoration,\nthe scheduler identity/path record and a tested undo. A program run does not\nprove worksheet completion or that a timer executed.\n',
	"LS3-Nginx-Reverse-Proxy-App":
		'# Run an application behind Nginx\n\nThis lab distinguishes the application socket, the front-end proxy socket and\nthe source of an HTTP response. Use Python 3, Nginx, curl and ss in an owned Linux\nlab. The app and proxy listen only on loopback high ports. The supplied unit is\nan optional user service, with the same ordinary account as the manual route.\nNeither source program installs software or changes a system-wide site.\n\n## Request contract and prediction\n\nBoth roles implement GET and HEAD /health: status 200 with service linux-toy-app\nand status ok. Unknown paths return JSON 404. POST returns JSON 405 with Allow:\nGET, HEAD. The request target is limited to 4096 characters and at most 16 query\nfields; excessive query fields return JSON 400. Content-Length counts UTF-8\nbytes, including the response newline; HEAD has the same headers without a body.\nAPP_PORT is a canonical ASCII integer from 1024 through 65535. Invalid settings\nexit 2 before binding; an occupied port exits 1. The default app port is 8081.\n\nGET /inspect records the actual path, parsed query, transport peer and four\nreceived headers. Repeated query keys remain lists and blank values remain\nvisible. For /inspect?mode=read&mode=write&blank=, predict a path of /inspect,\na two-element mode list and a one-element blank list containing an empty string.\nThe reference returns these observations. The starter deliberately returns\nJSON 501 at the named TODO; health is not proof that inspection is complete.\nComplete inspection_payload using its supplied arguments before comparing the\nreference. Do not replace observations with fixed sample answers.\n\n## Verify the application first\n\nExtract one role and this guide. Confirm uname -s, id and the permitted host\nscope. Check that ports 8081 and 8080 are free with ss -ltnp. If they are occupied,\nchoose two free high ports and change APP_PORT, proxy_pass and listen together.\nFrom the selected role, keep this process running in its own terminal:\n\n~~~sh\nAPP_PORT=8081 python3 app.py\n~~~\n\nIn a second terminal, record status, headers and body for these direct requests:\n\n~~~sh\ncurl -i http://127.0.0.1:8081/health\ncurl -i \'http://127.0.0.1:8081/inspect?mode=read&mode=write&blank=\'\ncurl -i http://127.0.0.1:8081/missing\ncurl -I http://127.0.0.1:8081/health\nss -ltnp \'sport = :8081\'\n~~~\n\nThe startup line is flushed immediately. Record the actual PID and socket\nidentity. A process listing alone does not establish healthy HTTP behavior.\nThe app reports header values as data; a client can supply forwarding headers\ndirectly. They are not authenticated identity or privilege evidence.\n\n## Run the selected Nginx block in a disposable prefix\n\nFrom the selected role, use this separate owned prefix. Keep the printed\nlab_root value for every later command. The heredoc expands only the selected\nabsolute include path; the supplied proxy block keeps its Nginx variables.\n\n~~~sh\nlab_root=$(mktemp -d)\nmkdir -p "$lab_root/logs"\ninstall -m 0644 linux-toy-app.conf "$lab_root/proxy.conf"\ncat > "$lab_root/nginx.conf" <<EOF\npid logs/nginx.pid;\nerror_log logs/main-error.log info;\nevents {}\nhttp {\n    access_log logs/main-access.log;\n    include "$lab_root/proxy.conf";\n}\nEOF\nnginx -p "$lab_root/" -c "$lab_root/nginx.conf" -t\nnginx -p "$lab_root/" -c "$lab_root/nginx.conf" -g \'daemon off;\'\n~~~\n\nKeep that Nginx process running in its terminal. In the request terminal:\n\n~~~sh\ncurl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/health\ncurl -i \'http://127.0.0.1:8080/inspect?mode=read&mode=write&blank=\'\ncurl -i -H \'Host: lab.invalid\' -H \'X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.9\' -H \'X-Forwarded-Proto: https\' \'http://127.0.0.1:8080/inspect?topic=ports\'\ncurl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/missing\nss -ltnp \'sport = :8080\'\ntail -n 10 "$lab_root/logs/proxy-access.log"\ntail -n 10 "$lab_root/logs/proxy-error.log"\n~~~\n\nThe two sockets are distinct. Without an added URI in proxy_pass, this block\nforwards the request path and query. The app\'s unknown-path 404 and the starter\'s\nunfinished 501 remain those statuses through the proxy. Nginx overwrites the\nsupplied forwarding headers with its observed loopback client and http scheme;\nthe supplied spoofed values do not become authentication evidence. Compare\nHost and received forwarding headers against the direct request. The access\nlog records request, status and upstream attempt; a log field alone does not\nprove that an app answered.\n\nStop the owned app with Ctrl-C while leaving the owned proxy running. A direct\nhealth request now fails to connect and a proxied health request returns 502.\nRead the proxy error log, restore the same app and verify recovery to 200.\nThis distinguishes an unavailable upstream from an app-produced 404 or 501.\nA syntax test only validates configuration; it neither starts Nginx nor proves\nthat the intended socket or application is serving.\n\n## Optional user-service route after foreground verification\n\nStop the foreground app before starting the same app port under a user manager.\nThe following route requires an available systemd user manager in the owned lab:\n\n~~~sh\nmkdir -p "$HOME/.local/share/linux-toy-app" "$HOME/.config/systemd/user"\ninstall -m 0644 app.py "$HOME/.local/share/linux-toy-app/app.py"\ninstall -m 0644 linux-toy-app.service "$HOME/.config/systemd/user/"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user start linux-toy-app.service\nsystemctl --user status linux-toy-app.service\njournalctl --user -u linux-toy-app.service --no-pager\ncurl -i http://127.0.0.1:8081/health\n~~~\n\nThe unit uses the user manager\'s home and active account, not www-data or a root\nHOME default. It needs no system-wide unit or /opt installation. Record actual\nmanager availability and inspect its working directory, environment and PID.\nIf the user bus is unavailable, retain the verified foreground route and record\nthat limitation; a parsed unit file is not an operating service. Restore any\npre-existing file before installing this lab under its name. The separate\nServices and systemd project covers sustained service-operation evidence.\n\n## Undo and completion record\n\nStop only the two foreground processes with Ctrl-C in their own terminals.\nFor the optional route, stop the named user service, restore or remove only its\nlab unit and app file, and reload the user manager. Confirm the two lab sockets\nare gone before removing the disposable prefix; preserve the logs until the\nfailure and recovery record is complete. No distribution default site is enabled,\nreplaced or claimed by this block.\n\nCompletion includes the inspection TODO repair, independent predictions and\nobservations, path/query and header comparisons, socket/PID evidence, the\nupstream-down 502 and recovery, and a verified undo. A reference run does not\ncomplete the record or establish public hosting or service-manager operation.\n\nPrimary references: [Python HTTP server](https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/http.server.html)\nand [Nginx proxy directives](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html).\n',
	"LS-13-unit-6-services-and-systemd-supplemental-2":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the guide shell variables.
		'# Transfer a working HTTP process to an owned user service\n\nThis optional Services and systemd exercise transfers the core service habits\nto a different, supplied working process. The named task is in the user unit:\nrepair its WorkingDirectory and ExecStart app argument. The program is reused\nfrom the accepted reverse-proxy app, with only its description changed. No\nNginx configuration or new HTTP handler implementation is required here.\nThe separate web-server project studies proxy behavior.\n\n## Prerequisites and predictions\n\nComplete the core toy-service project first. Use an owned Debian/Ubuntu Linux\nVM or systemd-enabled WSL lab with Python 3, curl, ss and a working user manager.\nCheck uname -s, id, ps -p 1 -o comm=, systemctl --version,\nsystemctl --user show --property=Version --value and journalctl --user.\nAn unavailable manager or inaccessible journal is an unmet lab prerequisite.\nA direct app run establishes a different contract from service operation.\n\nThe app accepts APP_PORT as an ASCII integer from 1024 through 65535, binds only\n127.0.0.1 and exposes GET/HEAD /health. Its exact UTF-8 GET body is:\n\n~~~text\n{"service": "linux-toy-app", "status": "ok"}\n~~~\n\nThe body ends with a newline; HEAD returns the same headers and no body.\n/inspect reports parsed query values and the selected headers, unknown paths\nreturn JSON 404, and POST returns 405 with Allow: GET, HEAD. Predict a repeated\nquery and a Unicode value before testing. Invalid APP_PORT exits 2 before\nbinding; an occupied port exits 1. Do not take over another process\'s port.\n\nThe untouched starter unit points at a missing working-directory child and a\nmissing Python script argument. These are two distinct tasks. After installation,\nthe first causes 200/CHDIR before Python. Repairing only WorkingDirectory exposes\nthe second failure: Python exits 2 and reports the missing script. Fix both\npaths to the installed root and transfer_app.py. The unit\'s prevent list stops\nthese configuration failures from becoming a restart loop. Type=simple start\nstatus is not the eventual service or HTTP-readiness result.\n\n## Install a unique unit and its app\n\nExtract one role and this reading, enter the selected role directory and keep\nthis shell open. The commands use the actual HOME and refuse an existing name.\nSelect a free owned loopback port; a later binding race still fails explicitly.\n\n~~~bash\nrole_dir=$(pwd -P)\nlab_name="linux-service-transfer-$(id -u)-$$"\nlab_unit="$lab_name.service"\ninstall_root="$HOME/.local/share/$lab_name"\nunit_root="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user"\nlab_port=$(python3 - <<\'PY\'\nimport socket\nwith socket.socket() as sock:\n    sock.bind(("127.0.0.1", 0))\n    print(sock.getsockname()[1])\nPY\n)\nif test ! -e "$install_root" &&\\\n   test ! -e "$unit_root/$lab_unit"; then\nmkdir -p "$install_root" "$unit_root"\ncp "$role_dir/transfer_app.py" "$install_root/transfer_app.py"\nprintf \'APP_PORT=%s\\n\' "$lab_port" >"$install_root/transfer.env"\npython3 - "$role_dir/linux-service-transfer.service" "$unit_root/$lab_unit" "$lab_name" <<\'PY\'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nsource, target, name = sys.argv[1:]\ntext = Path(source).read_text()\nprefix = "%h/.local/share/linux-service-transfer"\nassert text.count(prefix) == 3\nPath(target).write_text(text.replace(prefix, "%h/.local/share/" + name))\nPY\nsystemd-analyze --user verify "$unit_root/$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user start "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=LoadState,ActiveState,SubState,MainPID,ExecMainStatus,Result,NRestarts\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 30\nelse\n  printf \'The lab name is already in use. Stop here and choose a new name.\\n\' >&2\nfi\n~~~\n\n%h is the real user\'s home; this is a user unit inheriting that user\'s identity.\nDo not add a system User directive or substitute a different HOME. Explain each\ninstalled path, the required environment file, Python binary and restart choice.\nThe syntax check can pass while runtime paths fail. Record each distinct failure\nbefore correcting the named lines in the role unit, repeating the installation\nsubstitution, daemon-reload, reset-failed and start sequence.\n\n## Prove process and HTTP readiness\n\nFor the completed unit, inspect the owned PID and its actual listener, then\ncompare GET/HEAD with the predicted body and headers.\n\n~~~bash\nmain_pid=$(systemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=MainPID --value)\ntest "$main_pid" -gt 0\nps -p "$main_pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,args\nss -ltnp "sport = :$lab_port"\ncurl --fail-with-body "http://127.0.0.1:$lab_port/health"\ncurl --head "http://127.0.0.1:$lab_port/health"\ncurl --get --data-urlencode \'word=café\' --data-urlencode \'word=two\' "http://127.0.0.1:$lab_port/inspect"\n~~~\n\nConfirm active/running state, the installed app in the recorded process command,\nthe actual user UID, a 127.0.0.1 socket owned by that PID, the exact response and\njournal rows from that process. Another server\'s successful response is not\nthis unit\'s readiness. Record both command status and observed state.\n\n## Restart, failed-directory diagnosis and restoration\n\nRestart the unit, observe a new PID and repeat readiness. For an unexpected\nfailure, first reset-failed, re-read MainPID, verify its command/UID, then send\nSIGKILL only to that recorded process. Wait for a different running PID and an\nincreased NRestarts count; repeat the HTTP and journal checks. An explicit stop\nrequests termination and has a different meaning.\n\nKeep the working fragment unchanged during the next controlled fault. Add only\nthis owned drop-in, replacing the path with the unique install path above:\n\n~~~bash\nmkdir "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d"\nprintf \'[Service]\\nWorkingDirectory=%s/missing-directory\\n\' "$install_root" >"$unit_root/$lab_unit.d/90-lab-fault.conf"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user restart "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=ActiveState,MainPID,ExecMainStatus,Result\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 30\nrm "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d/90-lab-fault.conf"\nrmdir "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user start "$lab_unit"\n~~~\n\nRecord the actual missing-directory diagnostic and 200/CHDIR, then a fresh\nrunning process and healthy response after restoration. This fault occurs before\nthe app; an HTTP route or environment error is a different layer.\n\n## Enablement, record and cleanup\n\nEnable the unique unit, inspect its default.target.wants link, stop it and\nconfirm it remains enabled with MainPID=0. Complete service-transfer-notes.md\nwith predictions, observed failures, repairs, process/socket/journal evidence\nand the enable-versus-run distinction. A walkthrough can pause at each prediction\nand state transition; the same commands support independent work.\n\n~~~bash\nsystemctl --user enable "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user is-enabled "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user stop "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=ActiveState,MainPID,UnitFileState\nsystemctl --user disable "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"\nrm "$unit_root/$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=LoadState,ActiveState,MainPID\n~~~\n\nConfirm the process, listener, unit and activation link are gone. Retain the small\nowned install/evidence directory for review, then remove only that exact directory.\nThe reference supplies predictions and a completed unit; it does not supply\nobservations from the current lab.\n\nReferences: [systemd execution environment](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html),\n[service restart behavior](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.service.html).\n',
	"LS-14-unit-6-services-and-systemd-supplemental-3":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the guide shell variables.
		'# Extend an owned service with environment files and a drop-in\n\nThis optional Services and systemd exercise studies configuration precedence\nand restoration without rewriting the base unit. The heartbeat app is the\nworking reference from the core service project, used unchanged in both roles.\nThe named task is in 20-lab-settings.conf: clear the inherited environment-file\nlist and select override.env so actual process output uses extension-heartbeat.\nThis differs from transferring another app into a unit or implementing a heartbeat.\n\n## Prerequisites and the precedence model\n\nComplete the core toy-service project first. Use an owned Debian/Ubuntu Linux VM\nor systemd-enabled WSL lab with Python 3, a real user manager and readable user\njournal. Check uname -s, id, ps -p 1 -o comm=, systemctl --version,\nsystemctl --user show --property=Version --value and journalctl --user.\nResolve missing manager/journal access before claiming service completion.\n\nThe unit\'s Environment provides defaults. base.env replaces those defaults with\nbase-heartbeat and a 0.1-second interval. EnvironmentFile values take precedence\nover Environment, including an Environment assignment in a later drop-in.\nAn empty EnvironmentFile directive clears the accumulated file list; the next\nEnvironmentFile path selects override.env. Both files are required when selected.\nA missing file is not silently ignored. HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL accepts an ASCII\ndecimal from 0.1 through 60 without signs, exponents or padded integer parts.\nHEARTBEAT_MESSAGE is one nonempty UTF-8 line of at most 128 bytes; HEARTBEAT_LIMIT\nis 0 for continuous operation or a canonical integer from 1 through 9999.\nInvalid settings exit 2 before any heartbeat. From the role directory, a finite\ndirect check is:\n\n~~~bash\nHEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=0.1 HEARTBEAT_MESSAGE=sample HEARTBEAT_LIMIT=2 python3 heartbeat_app.py\n~~~\n\nThe exact output is sample: heartbeat 1 followed by sample: heartbeat 2, each\non its own newline-terminated row. This covers the app, not manager operation.\n\nThe untouched drop-in is syntactically valid and the service can remain healthy,\nyet actual output still uses base-heartbeat. This is the named unfinished\nconfiguration result. Complete only the file-list reset and replacement path,\nthen compare the reference after an independent completion. Predict every state\nand message before observing it. A healthy unit does not establish the intended\nconfiguration, and systemctl show Environment alone does not reveal file-supplied\nvalues in the running process.\n\n## Unique base installation\n\nExtract one role and this reading, enter the role directory and keep the shell\nopen. These commands read the actual HOME and refuse to replace an existing lab.\n\n~~~bash\nrole_dir=$(pwd -P)\nlab_name="linux-service-extension-$(id -u)-$$"\nlab_unit="$lab_name.service"\ninstall_root="$HOME/.local/share/$lab_name"\nunit_root="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user"\nif test ! -e "$install_root" &&\\\n   test ! -e "$unit_root/$lab_unit" &&\\\n   test ! -e "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d"; then\nmkdir -p "$install_root" "$unit_root"\ncp "$role_dir/heartbeat_app.py" "$install_root/heartbeat_app.py"\ncp "$role_dir/base.env.example" "$install_root/base.env"\ncp "$role_dir/override.env.example" "$install_root/override.env"\npython3 - "$role_dir/linux-service-extension.service" "$unit_root/$lab_unit" "$lab_name" <<\'PY\'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nsource, target, name = sys.argv[1:]\ntext = Path(source).read_text()\nprefix = "%h/.local/share/linux-service-extension"\nassert text.count(prefix) == 3\nPath(target).write_text(text.replace(prefix, "%h/.local/share/" + name))\nPY\nsha256sum "$unit_root/$lab_unit" >"$install_root/base-unit.sha256"\nsystemd-analyze --user verify "$unit_root/$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user start "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=ActiveState,SubState,MainPID,EnvironmentFiles,FragmentPath,DropInPaths\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 20\nelse\n  printf \'The lab name is already in use. Stop here and choose a new name.\\n\' >&2\nfi\n~~~\n\nConfirm an owned running PID and base-heartbeat rows from that process. %h resolves\nto the actual user\'s home, and the user unit inherits that user\'s identity.\nVerify /usr/bin/python3 and the installed app path in this Debian/Ubuntu lab.\nThe app\'s interval/message/limit validation is described in the core reading;\nthese examples use continuous service operation with HEARTBEAT_LIMIT=0.\n\n## Install the selected role\'s drop-in\n\n~~~bash\nmkdir "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d"\npython3 - "$role_dir/20-lab-settings.conf" "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d/20-lab-settings.conf" "$lab_name" <<\'PY\'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nsource, target, name = sys.argv[1:]\ntext = Path(source).read_text()\nPath(target).write_text(text.replace("%h/.local/share/linux-service-extension", "%h/.local/share/" + name))\nPY\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user cat "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=EnvironmentFiles,DropInPaths,NeedDaemonReload\nsystemctl --user restart "$lab_unit"\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 30\nsha256sum --check "$install_root/base-unit.sha256"\n~~~\n\nRe-read MainPID after restart and correlate only that PID\'s journal rows with\nthe predicted message. The untouched drop-in keeps base-heartbeat. The reference\nand independent completion select only override.env and produce extension-heartbeat.\nInspect the owned process command/UID and, when readable, its actual environment:\n\n~~~bash\nmain_pid=$(systemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=MainPID --value)\nps -p "$main_pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,args\ntr \'\\0\' \'\\n\' <"/proc/$main_pid/environ" | grep \'^HEARTBEAT_\'\n~~~\n\nExplain why the merged unit\'s Environment assignment can differ from actual\nprocess values. Record the effective file list and unchanged base-unit hash.\n\n## File edits, restart, failure and rollback\n\nKeep the working override file, then change its message to edited-heartbeat\nwithout changing the unit or drop-in. Observe that the current PID and its rows\nretain the previous message. Restart, then confirm a different PID and fresh\nedited-heartbeat rows. File contents are read for the next process; a running\nprocess does not acquire edits. Unit/drop-in directive edits additionally need\ndaemon-reload. A reload command is not an app restart.\n\nFor the completed configuration, back up override.env and set its interval to 0.\nRestart and observe a failed unit, MainPID=0, exit 2 and an actual interval\ndiagnostic; the prevent list avoids a retry loop. Restore valid contents,\nreset-failed, start and confirm recovery with a new PID and expected rows.\nThe untouched starter still selects base.env, so an ignored override-file edit\nis evidence of its unfinished task, not successful extension.\n\nRemove only the owned 20-lab-settings.conf, remove its now-empty directory,\ndaemon-reload, reset-failed and restart. Observe only base.env in EnvironmentFiles,\na new owned PID and base-heartbeat output. Confirm the base fragment hash remains\nunchanged. Record both restoration and cleanup in service-extension-notes.md.\n\n~~~bash\nrm "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d/20-lab-settings.conf"\nrmdir "$unit_root/$lab_unit.d"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user restart "$lab_unit"\nsha256sum --check "$install_root/base-unit.sha256"\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=MainPID,EnvironmentFiles,DropInPaths\njournalctl --user -u "$lab_unit" --no-pager -n 20\nsystemctl --user stop "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user disable "$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$lab_unit"\nrm "$unit_root/$lab_unit"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user show "$lab_unit" --property=LoadState,MainPID\n~~~\n\nConfirm the owned unit, drop-in, activation link and process are gone. Retain\nsource/evidence for review, then remove only the unique installed directory.\nAn analysis record requires the actual observations above; completed source or a\nprinted prediction does not replace them. The same ordered predictions and\ncommands support an independent reading or a paced walkthrough.\n\nReferences: [environment-file precedence](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html),\n[unit drop-ins](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.unit.html).\n',
	"LS-09-unit-7-logging-and-observability":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve literal operating commands.
		'# Unit 7: Logging and Observability\n\n## Core project: reconstruct and repair an owned service incident\n\nThe operating question is why one named service failed, which evidence supports\nthat cause, and whether a later invocation completed after the correction. This\nis a journal investigation, separate from shell file enumeration. The optional\ntransfer investigates application records; the optional extension measures file\nretention. The notebook uses this same core attempt and records its reasoning.\n\nThe source includes a deliberately broken incident fixture, a service unit,\nredacted journal records and an analyzer. The learner task is `in_scope` inside\n`task.sh`: select the requested user unit, boot ID and inclusive microsecond\nwindow. The supplied parsing and report code then orders records, counts error\npriorities and distinguishes a later ready event in a new invocation from a\nrepeated or unrelated message. The reference completes the same task.\n\nThe embedded Python receives each JSON object as a dictionary named row.\n`row.get("FIELD")` reads a field without treating an absent field as a match;\nthe two unit-field alternatives refer to application and manager records.\nA manager entry can carry `_SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT=init.scope` and\n`USER_UNIT=linux-observe-demo.service` together. Select the requested service\nwhen either field matches, then also require the boot and time window. A\nfirst-present-field fallback loses relevant manager entries. Python `or`\ncombines the unit alternatives; `and` combines the required scope conditions.\n`int` converts the validated decimal timestamp for numeric comparisons. The\nselection returns a boolean: all requested scope conditions hold together.\nReturning None marks the named unfinished task rather than selecting a row.\n\n## Concepts and limits\n\n`systemctl --user status UNIT` describes the current invocation; `journalctl`\nretains events from older invocations. A failed start followed by an active\noneshot fixture does not mean the earlier failure never happened. A boot ID and\ntime window prevent an old incident from being mistaken for the current one.\n`__REALTIME_TIMESTAMP` uses epoch microseconds. `PRIORITY` uses syslog numbers:\n0 through 3 are emergency through error; 4 is warning and 6 is information.\nThe word ERROR in an informational message does not change its priority.\n\nUser-unit journal records may identify the unit with `_SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT` or\n`USER_UNIT`; application records and service-manager explanations have different\nresponsibilities. First identify the application diagnostic, then corroborate\nit with the service exit status. `config_missing` means the declared file is\nabsent; `config_invalid` means the file exists but its content is unsupported.\nA later `ready` event is evidence of a completed fixture invocation. The report retains the first and latest application errors. A later failure\nreopens the case: recovery requires a newer ready event in a different\ninvocation after that latest failure. Check current service state independently.\n\nThe analyzer accepts JSON-per-line records with scalar journal fields. Missing\nfiles, invalid JSON, unsupported field shapes and invalid windows fail with\nstatus 2. An empty valid selection produces an empty evidence report, which is\nnot proof of health. The untouched starter diagnoses its unfinished task with\nstatus 78. No private machine logs are supplied or required for the fixture.\n\n## Read, predict and complete\n\nFrom the selected role directory, read `sample.log` and predict the records\nincluded by this query before completing `in_scope`:\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh sample.log linux-observe-demo.service aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa 100 500\n~~~\n\nThe four application case events, one manager explanation and the informational\nERROR-word message belong to this window: six records, including two with error\npriority. The manager explanation has USER_UNIT naming the target while its own\n_SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT is init.scope. The other boot, other unit and timestamp 50 do not. The application\ncause is `case: event=config_missing` at 200, and a different invocation records\n`case: event=ready` at 400. Count priorities rather than substrings. Change the\nwindow to end at 300: recovery disappears. Change the unit or boot: the\nselection is empty. Remove the input file or damage a copy of its JSON:\nthat must fail visibly rather than print a successful zero summary. Preserve\nthe supplied source when making changed fixtures.\n\n## Real Linux investigation\n\nUse an owned Linux/WSL environment with Bash, Python 3, a working user systemd\nmanager and readable user journal. `systemctl --user show-environment` and\n`journalctl --user -n 1 --no-pager` are readiness checks. If the user journal is\nunavailable, complete the redacted route and arrange a supported lab; do not\nchange a shared machine\'s journal policy or claim live acceptance. macOS is not\na systemd host. The site workspace can edit/save/download these files, while\nthese operating commands run in the Linux terminal.\n\nCreate a private disposable lab and install a uniquely named fixture unit. The\nconfiguration is initially absent, so the first start has a known failure. Run\nfrom the selected role directory:\n\n~~~sh\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/linux-observe.XXXXXX")\nunit="linux-observe-$(basename "$lab").service"\nunit_path="$HOME/.config/systemd/user/$unit"\nmkdir -p "$HOME/.config/systemd/user"\ncp incident_app.sh "$lab/incident_app.sh"\npython3 - linux-logging-case.service "$lab" "$unit_path" <<\'PYTHON\'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nsource, lab, destination = sys.argv[1:]\nif any(character in lab for character in \'\\n\\r"\\\\\'):\n    raise SystemExit("Use a lab path without quotes, backslashes or line breaks.")\nwith Path(destination).open(\'x\') as target:\n    target.write(Path(source).read_text().replace(\'__LAB__\', lab))\nPYTHON\nstart_us=$(date +%s%6N)\nboot=$(cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id | tr -d -)\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user start "$unit" || test "$(systemctl --user show "$unit" -p ExecMainStatus --value)" = 2\nsystemctl --user status "$unit" --no-pager || true\njournalctl --user -u "$unit" -b --no-pager -o json >"$lab/failed.log"\n~~~\n\nThe first relevant application event says `config_missing`; the manager\'s\n`ExecMainStatus=2` corroborates the exit. Record both before changing anything.\nRepair only the fixture\'s declared file, then start a new invocation:\n\n~~~sh\nprintf \'enabled=yes\\n\' >"$lab/case.conf"\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$unit"\nsystemctl --user start "$unit"\nsystemctl --user show "$unit" -p ActiveState -p SubState -p ExecMainStatus\njournalctl --user -u "$unit" -b --no-pager -o json >"$lab/incident.log"\nend_us=$(date +%s%6N)\nbash task.sh "$lab/incident.log" "$unit" "$boot" "$start_us" "$end_us"\n~~~\n\nExpect active/exited and status 0 for this oneshot fixture. Check that the ready\nrow has a later timestamp and a new `_SYSTEMD_INVOCATION_ID`; retain minimal\nredacted excerpts in `incident-notes.md`. Do not infer a continuously running\nprocess from an exited oneshot. Try `enabled=no`, observe `config_invalid` and\nstatus 2, then restore `enabled=yes` and retest. Never delete broad journal\nhistory to reset an exercise. Only the fixture unit and private lab are cleaned:\n\n~~~sh\nsystemctl --user stop "$unit"\nrm -- "$unit_path"\nsystemctl --user daemon-reload\nsystemctl --user reset-failed "$unit" 2>/dev/null || true\ncase "$lab" in */linux-observe.*) rm -r -- "$lab" ;; esac\n~~~\n\n## Review and completion evidence\n\nExplain the selected scope, first trustworthy diagnostic, why the word ERROR\nand unrelated records were rejected, the specific correction, fresh retest,\ncurrent state and cleanup. Independent reading and paced discussion use\nthe same sequence: predict, select, explain, change one case, retest. Reference copies remain separate from saved attempts. Compare after an\nattempted draft. Full native acceptance exercises the actual user manager and journal,\nall three source roles, missing/invalid configuration, new invocations and\ncleanup; the redacted route alone does not establish that operating result.\n\nReferences: [journalctl](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/journalctl.html)\nand [journal fields](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.journal-fields.html).\n',
	"LS-15-unit-7-logging-and-observability-supplemental-2":
		"# Log Investigation Transfer Practice\n\nThis optional project transfers the core investigation habit to structured\napplication records. It is distinct from the user-service journal project:\nrequest identity, completion event and numeric HTTP status answer whether one\nrequest failed and whether a later completed retry recovered. An application\nmessage is not a service-manager exit result, and a recorded 200 does not prove\nthe current service is available.\n\n## Supplied source and learner task\n\n`sample.log` contains redacted JSON-per-line events for case-7 plus an unrelated\nrequest. `task.sh` supplies input validation, explicit timezone handling,\nchronological ordering and reporting. Complete `is_server_failure`: a server\nfailure is a completed response whose numeric status is from 500 through 599.\nStarting a request is not a response, 400 is a client response rather than a\nserver failure, and a message containing ERROR is not itself a classification.\nThe embedded Python receives a dictionary named row. Square-bracket access\nreads an already validated field. Check the completion event before reading\nstatus: start records have no response status. Python and short-circuits, so\nits right-hand condition is evaluated only when its left-hand condition holds.\nThe reference completes the same named function; `application-notes.md` records\ncausal reasoning and limitations. No network request is performed by this pack.\n\n## Predict, run and explain\n\nFrom the selected role directory:\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh sample.log case-7\n~~~\n\nThe untouched starter exits 78 with its named task. After completion, case-7\nhas four events, responses 503 then 200, exactly one server failure and a later\nrecorded recovery. Recovery is measured after the latest server failure; a\nnewer 503 reopens the case until a later completed 200. The other request's 500 is excluded. A 200 before a later\n503 does not recover that later failure. A request start without a completion leaves\nlastStatus null and cannot establish success. UTC offsets are compared as\ninstants, not as strings; each timestamp needs a timezone. Missing input,\ninvalid JSON, a string or boolean status, or an invalid timestamp fails with\nstatus 2 rather than being silently converted to a zero count.\n\nCopy the supplied records to a temporary file before changed-case work. Test a\n400 response, a 599 response, a different request, shuffled input order, an\nincomplete retry and timestamps expressed with equivalent UTC offsets. Record\npredictions before running each changed case. Keep a malformed-input failure\nseparate from a valid empty request selection.\n\n## Review and boundaries\n\nUse the notebook to state the symptom, chosen request, ordered response\nstatuses, first failure, later completion, excluded noise, remaining unknowns\nand one changed-case result. The source and command sequence support both\nindependent reading and a paced walkthrough. Compare the reference after\nan attempted draft; preserve every earlier saved attempt. Only supplied\nor explicitly redacted records are needed, and no production log collection\nor private data upload is part of this project. The site IDE supports source\nediting, save/reopen and exact ZIP export; execute this Bash/Python tool in the\nLinux terminal. No pip dependency is required.\n",
	"LS-16-unit-7-logging-and-observability-supplemental-3":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve literal operating commands.
		'# Log Investigation Extension Practice\n\nThis optional extension measures a retention policy on disposable application\nfiles with the real logrotate program. It follows investigation work but adds\nan operating question: which batches remain after rotation, how are they read,\nand can the application write to the newly created active file? It does not\nvacuum a system journal, alter system logrotate jobs or schedule a new service.\n\n## Source and task\n\n`task.sh` is a supplied renderer and runner for exactly one owned application\nlog. `logrotate.conf` is the learner task: replace TODO with a policy retaining\ntwo archives. The untouched starter produces logrotate\'s bad-count failure;\nno valid rotation is claimed. The reference uses `rotate 2`. `size 1` provides\na small disposable trigger; `--force` makes these manual tests reproducible.\n`compress` stores rotated batches in gzip files. `create 0600` creates a private\nnew active file, `notifempty` skips empty input and `missingok` tolerates an\nabsent file in the policy. The supplied runner deliberately requires app.log\nfor the lab so an unintended missing input remains visible.\n\nThe directory must belong to the active user, have mode 700 and contain the\nexact lab marker. Its app.log, generated policy and state paths must be regular\nfiles rather than symlinks. The runner confines its generated template path to\nthis directory. Spaces in a directory name are supported; quotes, backslashes\nand line breaks are rejected explicitly. Bash, Python 3, GNU stat, gzip and\nlogrotate are required in the owned Linux environment. The site IDE edits and\nexports this pack; operating commands run in that Linux terminal.\n\n## Predict, preview and rotate\n\nRun from the selected role directory. Create a new private lab and give it a\nmarker that distinguishes it from other folders:\n\n~~~sh\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/linux-log-retention.XXXXXX")\nchmod 700 "$lab"\nprintf \'linux-log-retention-v1\\n\' >"$lab/.linux-log-lab"\nprintf \'batch-one\\n\' >"$lab/app.log"\nlogrotate --version\nbash task.sh "$lab"\n~~~\n\nAfter completing the policy, one run creates app.log.1.gz containing batch-one\nand a new empty app.log with mode 600 as reported by GNU stat. Inspect the\nrendered policy and use logrotate\'s debug mode as a read-only preview of future\noperations; debug mode does not rotate files or update the state file:\n\n~~~sh\ncat "$lab/rotation.conf"\nlogrotate --debug --state "$lab/rotation.state" "$lab/rotation.conf"\ngzip -cd -- "$lab/app.log.1.gz"\nstat -c \'%a %U %n\' -- "$lab/app.log"\nprintf \'batch-two\\n\' >>"$lab/app.log"\nbash task.sh "$lab"\nprintf \'batch-three\\n\' >>"$lab/app.log"\nbash task.sh "$lab"\ngzip -cd -- "$lab/app.log.1.gz"\ngzip -cd -- "$lab/app.log.2.gz"\n~~~\n\nPredict before each operation. After three nonempty rotations, the newest\narchive contains batch-three, the second contains batch-two and batch-one has\nexpired under the count-two policy. Count archives and verify their contents;\na successful command alone does not prove retention. Run again with the empty\nactive file: notifempty leaves the archive identities and contents\nunchanged. Write a new batch to the active file to show that creation and\nownership permit subsequent logging.\n\nPlace an unrelated sentinel outside the lab and verify its hash remains\nunchanged. Try a missing marker or a symlinked app.log in a separate disposable\ncopy: the runner must reject those inputs before calling logrotate. Try a path\ncontaining spaces. The lab state file is local to this exercise; never pass a\nsystem state file or /etc/logrotate.conf to these commands.\n\n## Cleanup and evidence\n\nRecord the policy, version, preview, actual archive contents, expiration,\nactive-file mode, empty-run result, sentinel hash and cleanup in\n`retention-notes.md`. Remove only the directory created above:\n\n~~~sh\ncase "$lab" in\n    */linux-log-retention.*)\n        test "$(cat -- "$lab/.linux-log-lab")" = linux-log-retention-v1 && rm -r -- "$lab"\n        ;;\nesac\n~~~\n\nThe independent and paced routes share the prediction/operation/inspection\nsequence. Compare the reference after completing the policy draft; preserve a\nseparate saved attempt. Native acceptance must use actual logrotate,\nnot simulated archive names. This exercise observes manually invoked retention;\nit does not claim that a scheduler or running application reopened a file.\nReference: [logrotate manual](https://github.com/logrotate/logrotate/blob/main/logrotate.8.in).\n',
	"LS-07-unit-4-editing-and-configuration":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the accepted guide's literal shell variables.
		'# Unit 4: A validated configuration edit and rollback\n\nThis is the required Editing and Configuration project. The troubleshooting\nnotebook belongs to the same attempt. Optional environment-file transfer and\ncontrolled-replacement work have separate purposes and are not prerequisites\nfor completing this core project.\n\nThe outcome is an observed operating loop: inspect, back up, edit, validate,\ncompare, apply, reload, observe and roll back. A successful file write alone is\nnot evidence that a running program accepted the change. This pack provides a\nsmall process so the distinction can be tested without changing a system\nservice, opening a network listener, using root or editing /etc.\n\n## Environment and files\n\nUse an owned Linux VM, WSL distribution or Linux terminal with Bash, Python 3.9 or later,\nGNU coreutils, diff and a terminal editor. This workflow uses GNU realpath and\nchmod --reference; the complete shell workflow is not a macOS compatibility\nclaim. On an owned Debian/Ubuntu lab, missing tools can be installed with\n`sudo apt-get install bash python3 coreutils diffutils nano`. No package install\nis needed when these commands already exist.\n\nBegin in this pack\'s `starter` directory. The `solution` directory contains one\nworking reference for later comparison. Each role includes:\n\n- `config.json`: the valid baseline, with message `Baseline ready` and repeat 1.\n- `candidate.json`: the proposed edit. The starter\'s repeat 9 is deliberately\n  invalid; the reference uses repeat 2.\n- `config_app.py`: the complete validator and disposable process. This is\n  supplied operating equipment, not an additional Python assignment.\n- `task.sh`: initialization, the named unfinished apply step and working\n  rollback. Only the starter\'s apply section requires implementation.\n- `edit-notes.md`: predictions, observations, diagnosis and recovery evidence.\n\nJSON uses double-quoted keys and strings, commas between entries, and no\ncomments or trailing comma. The application accepts exactly `message` and\n`repeat`. Message must contain 1 through 80 characters on one line. Repeat must\nbe an integer from 1 through 5; a string, boolean, duplicate key or unknown key\nis rejected. Syntax validation and these application value rules are different\nchecks. An editor can save a syntactically valid file whose repeat is still 9.\n\n## 1. Inspect and predict\n\n~~~sh\npwd\nsed -n \'1,120p\' task.sh\ncat config.json candidate.json\npython3 config_app.py validate config.json\npython3 config_app.py validate candidate.json\n~~~\n\nThe baseline prints `configuration valid` and exits 0. The untouched starter\ncandidate is rejected with exit 2 because repeat is outside its allowed range.\nRun `echo "$?"` immediately after the command when recording an exit status.\nPredict the result before running it. A command\'s visible text and its exit\nstatus are both evidence.\n\n## 2. Create one disposable lab\n\n~~~sh\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/unit4-edit.XXXXXX")\nbash task.sh init "$lab"\nprintf \'LAB=%s\\n\' "$lab"\nls -la "$lab"\ncp -p "$lab/config.json" "$lab/baseline.evidence.json"\n~~~\n\nInitialization requires an empty directory owned by the current account and\ncreates an ownership marker plus the two working configuration files. It\nrefuses a symbolic-link lab directory and later refuses symbolic links or\nnon-owned files at its three managed configuration paths. Keep the printed\nabsolute lab path. Never substitute a real service directory. The evidence\ncopy is independent of the controller\'s rolling `config.before.json` backup.\n\n## 3. Observe a real running process\n\nIn terminal A, from the same role directory, run:\n\n~~~sh\npython3 config_app.py run "$lab/config.json"\n~~~\n\nIt prints one JSON record with event `started`, its actual process ID, message,\nrepeat and rendered output. The baseline renders `Baseline ready` once. Leave\nthis terminal running. In terminal B, change to that same role directory and\nassign `lab` to the exact printed LAB path, and `pid` to the actual PID reported\nby this one disposable process. Replace the example text before running:\n\n~~~sh\nlab=\'/exact/LAB/path/printed/in/terminal/A\'\npid=12345  # replace with this lab process\'s reported PID\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\n~~~\n\nUse only the PID of the process just started for this lab. A signal is a small\nnotification to a process. Here USR1 requests a snapshot, HUP requests reload,\nand INT or TERM requests exit. These meanings are implemented by this supplied\nprogram; they are not universal meanings for every application. Terminal A\nprints a `snapshot` record. Its PID stays the same throughout reload and\nrollback, demonstrating an operating process rather than repeated fresh runs.\n\nThe program parses its file at startup and caches the accepted settings. Its\nsignal handlers request work, and the main loop validates a replacement before\nswapping the cached settings. A rejected reload reports its reason and keeps\nthe previous accepted settings. Reading `rendered` shows the settings\' effect\non the program\'s actual output.\n\n## 4. Edit the candidate and inspect the difference\n\nUse `nano "$lab/candidate.json"` or `vim "$lab/candidate.json"`. Set message to\n`Edited configuration` and repeat to 2. In nano, Ctrl+O saves, Enter confirms the\nfilename, and Ctrl+X exits. In vim, press i to insert, Escape to leave insertion,\nthen type `:wq` and Enter to save and exit. `:q!` exits without saving.\n\n~~~sh\npython3 config_app.py validate "$lab/candidate.json"\ndiff -u "$lab/config.json" "$lab/candidate.json"\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\n~~~\n\nValidation passes with these values. Diff exits 1 when the two files differ; that is not\na failed comparison command. Inspect the changed keys before continuing. The\nrunning process still reports the baseline because editing the candidate does\nnot apply it. Quoting every path keeps a path containing spaces one argument.\n\n## 5. Complete the starter\'s named apply step\n\nFirst run `bash task.sh apply "$lab"`. The untouched starter reports\n`unfinished: implement the validated apply step`, exits 78 and changes no\nconfiguration or backup. This deliberate result is not a completed exercise.\n\nReplace only the block between APPLY_BEGIN and APPLY_END in the starter\'s\n`task.sh`. Implement these operations in order:\n\n1. Run this role\'s Python validator on both the current file and candidate.\n   Do not replace a known-good backup with an already invalid current file.\n2. Copy current config to `config.before.json`, preserving its mode with cp -p.\n3. Create a temporary file inside the lab with mktemp. Register an EXIT trap\n   that removes that exact temporary path if any later step fails.\n4. Copy the candidate to the temporary file and use chmod --reference with the\n   current config to retain its mode.\n5. Move the prepared file over `config.json`. Keeping the temporary file in the\n   same directory allows the rename to replace that one directory entry\n   atomically. It does not turn a whole multi-file deployment into a transaction.\n\nThe supplied variables are `$lab`, `$app` and `$here`. Invoke validation as\n`python3 "$app" validate "$lab/candidate.json"`, with the corresponding current\npath for the first check. Use `cp -p -- SOURCE DESTINATION`,\n`staged=$(mktemp "$lab/.config.XXXXXX")`,\n`trap \'rm -f -- "$staged"\' EXIT`,\n`chmod --reference="$lab/config.json" "$staged"` and\n`mv -f -- "$staged" "$lab/config.json"` as the relevant shell building blocks.\nThe variables inside the single-quoted trap are expanded when the trap runs.\n\n`set -euo pipefail` stops on an unhandled failed command, rejects an unset\nvariable and propagates a failed pipeline component. It does not validate\nconfiguration semantics for the program. `umask 077` restricts newly created\nfiles. `$BASH_SOURCE` locates the controller\'s supplied validator independently\nof the current working directory. The guards and rollback are provided so the\nassignment focuses on the validated apply ordering.\n\n## 6. Apply, then explicitly reload\n\n~~~sh\nbash -n task.sh\nbash task.sh apply "$lab"\ncmp "$lab/config.before.json" "$lab/baseline.evidence.json"\ncmp "$lab/config.json" "$lab/candidate.json"\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\nkill -HUP "$pid"\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\n~~~\n\nBash -n checks shell syntax without executing the script. Both cmp commands\nexit 0, proving exact backup and applied bytes. The snapshot before HUP\nstill shows the baseline. Terminal A then reports `reloaded`, followed by a\nsnapshot rendering `Edited configuration Edited configuration`. Copying a file\nand asking an application to reload it are separate operations.\n\n## 7. Reject a changed case and prove rollback\n\nSave exact copies of the active file and backup as evidence. Change the\ncandidate\'s repeat to 0, or add a second repeat key, and run apply again. It\nmust exit nonzero. Compare active and backup to the evidence copies; both must\nremain byte-for-byte unchanged. Request a snapshot and confirm the process\nstill renders the accepted edited message twice.\n\n~~~sh\ncp -p "$lab/config.json" "$lab/active.evidence.json"\ncp -p "$lab/config.before.json" "$lab/backup.evidence.json"\n# Edit candidate.json to an invalid changed case before running the next line.\nbash task.sh apply "$lab"\ncmp "$lab/config.json" "$lab/active.evidence.json"\ncmp "$lab/config.before.json" "$lab/backup.evidence.json"\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\nbash task.sh rollback "$lab"\ncmp "$lab/config.json" "$lab/baseline.evidence.json"\nkill -HUP "$pid"\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\n~~~\n\nThe final running output must again render `Baseline ready` once with the same\nPID. Repeat with a different valid message containing spaces and repeat 3.\nPredict the resulting rendered text. For an additional recovery investigation,\nretain valid evidence, deliberately make only this disposable active file\ninvalid, and request HUP. Observe `reload-rejected` and the previous accepted\nrunning output, then use the validated rollback and reload to restore it.\n\n## Evidence, comparison and cleanup\n\nComplete edit-notes.md with commands, predicted and observed results, exit\nstatuses, exact comparisons, the real PID, rejected change and final recovery.\nPause after each numbered section for discussion, or work through\nthe same sequence independently. Explain why validation precedes replacement\nand why editing disk did not itself update the running output. Compare the\nreference only after producing a working attempt and explain any differences.\n\nPress Ctrl+C in terminal A and observe `stopped`. Confirm the lab process has\nexited before removing only the exact disposable directory created above:\n`rm -rf -- "$lab"`. Preserve the worksheet and any required evidence first.\nDo not use an unrelated PID or a real configuration directory for cleanup.\n\nThe source verifier runs the starter, reference and an independent completion\nagainst actual owned processes. Its Linux acceptance is retained separately\nfrom site import/export acceptance. A local syntax check alone proves neither.\n',
	"LS-09-unit-4-editing-and-configuration-supplemental-2":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the accepted guide's literal shell variables.
		'# Optional transfer: environment files and actual child processes\n\nComplete the required configuration-edit core first. This optional transfer\nasks a different question: which settings does a new process actually inherit,\nand what happens when its assignment file changes after launch? Keep a separate\nattempt and environment-notes.md. The core troubleshooting notebook continues\nthe core attempt rather than becoming another copy of this task.\n\n## Setup, format and supplied equipment\n\nUse an owned Linux terminal with Bash, Python 3.9 or later and GNU realpath.\nNo service manager, root access or package install is needed when these tools\nalready exist. Begin in `starter`; compare `solution` after completing an\nattempt. This pack contains task.sh, settings.env, environment_app.py and the\nevidence worksheet in each role.\n\nThe settings file is specifically a trusted **Bash assignment file**. It is\nloaded by Bash\'s source builtin, so it can execute shell code. Use only the\nowned demonstration assignments supplied and edited for this lab. This is not\na generic dotenv parser or systemd EnvironmentFile reader; those formats have\ntheir own rules. There is no automatic variable substitution specification\nshared by every file with an .env suffix.\n\nThe supplied file assigns `MESSAGE=\'Environment baseline\'` and `REPEAT=2` but\ndoes not export them. Single quotes keep the space inside MESSAGE and are\nremoved by the shell when it creates the value. Shell variable assignment and\nenvironment export are separate operations. An exported variable is included\nwhen a shell starts a child; an ordinary shell variable need not be.\n\nThe supplied application reads only MESSAGE and REPEAT from its own\nos.environ. Message must contain 1 through 80 characters on one line. Repeat\nmust be one digit from 1 through 5. The application reports the actual PID, UID,\nsettings and rendered repetition. Use demonstration text in MESSAGE because\nthat value is printed. The application code is supplied; the learner task is\nthe named export section in task.sh.\n\n## 1. Predict and make a disposable copy\n\n~~~sh\ncat settings.env\nbash -n settings.env\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/unit4-env.XXXXXX")\ncp -- settings.env "$lab/settings.env"\nprintf \'LAB=%s\\n\' "$lab"\nbash task.sh "$lab/settings.env"\necho "$?"\n~~~\n\nBash -n checks shell syntax without running assignments. The untouched starter\nreports `unfinished: export MESSAGE and REPEAT before launching the child`,\nexits 78 and starts no application. That named unfinished outcome is not a\ncompleted transfer. The launcher accepts an owned regular settings file and\nrefuses symbolic links. It resolves the filename before starting the child.\n\n## 2. Complete the export section\n\nEdit task.sh with nano or vim. Replace only the block between EXPORT_BEGIN and\nEXPORT_END. After the supplied `source "$1"` line, export exactly MESSAGE and\nREPEAT before launching Python. `export NAME OTHER_NAME` marks current shell\nvariables for inheritance. Leave the provided clean-environment launch, option\nhandling and exec statements intact. Check the script with `bash -n task.sh`.\n\nThe launcher uses `env -i PATH="$PATH"` to start a deliberately clean shell,\nretaining only the command-search path. That makes the test independent of\nMESSAGE or REPEAT already exported in the terminal. It does not erase the\nparent terminal\'s environment. `bash --noprofile --norc` skips user startup\nfiles. The child sources this lab\'s file, exports the named settings, applies\nany explicit message override, then execs Python. Exec replaces that child\nprocess rather than adding another long-running launcher.\n\n`set -euo pipefail` handles failed commands, unset-variable use and pipeline\nfailures. It does not make Bash -n an application value validator. Python checks\nthe two values only after receiving the child environment.\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh "$lab/settings.env"\nMESSAGE=\'Parent-only value\' REPEAT=5 bash task.sh "$lab/settings.env"\nbash task.sh "$lab/settings.env" --message \'Explicit override\'\n~~~\n\nPredict each result. The first two calls both report Environment baseline and\nrepeat 2 because the child is deliberately cleaned before sourcing the file.\nThe third reports Explicit override and repeat 2: the explicit launcher option\noverrides MESSAGE after sourcing, without changing the file. The rendered\nstring contains the chosen message twice. These are actual process environment\nvalues, not text extracted from the file by the verifier.\n\nTo distinguish parent and child state, record the terminal\'s\n`printf \'%s\\n\' "${MESSAGE-unset}" "${REPEAT-unset}"` before and after a call.\nStarting this child does not export its variables back into the parent.\n\n## 3. Observe file edits versus a running environment\n\nIn terminal A, leave a watched child running:\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh "$lab/settings.env" --watch\n~~~\n\nIt prints a started record with its actual PID. In terminal B, change to the\nsame role directory, set lab to the exact printed LAB path and pid to this\nchild\'s reported PID. Use only the process just started for this lab. Edit the\nowned settings file to `MESSAGE=\'Changed disk value\'` and `REPEAT=3`, then run:\n\n~~~sh\nbash -n "$lab/settings.env"\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\n~~~\n\nThe application implements USR1 as a snapshot request. It still reports\nEnvironment baseline and repeat 2. Editing a file does not retroactively alter\nthe environment of an already running process. No reload feature is claimed\nfor this application. Press Ctrl+C in terminal A; it reports stopped. Start\nthe same launcher again and observe Changed disk value with repeat 3 and a new\nPID. Record the actual two process identities and outputs.\n\n## 4. Separate syntax failure from value failure\n\nAfter stopping the watched child, retain a copy of the valid settings file.\nTry these changes one at a time in the disposable copy:\n\n- An unmatched quote: Bash -n fails, and the launcher stops before application\n  launch. Repair the quote before the next case.\n- `REPEAT=9`: Bash -n succeeds, but the launched application reports\n  `invalid environment` and exits 2. Syntax validity is not value validity.\n- Remove MESSAGE: the fresh child cannot obtain that setting from the caller;\n  the application exits 2. Restore the assignment afterward.\n- `MESSAGE=\'A different quoted choice\'` with REPEAT=3: predict the three-copy\n  rendered output, run it and compare. Verify that the quote characters are not\n  part of the inherited value.\n- An empty explicit `--message \'\'` override: the application rejects its value\n  rather than treating it as no override.\n\nThe settings file remains unchanged when an explicit launcher override is\nused. Keep the complete error text and immediate exit status for each changed\ncase. Do not interpret an application validation failure as a Bash parser\nfailure, or infer a file reload from a new process launch.\n\n## Evidence and cleanup\n\nComplete environment-notes.md with predictions, actual child values, PIDs/UIDs,\nparent-state observations, file changes, restart results, overrides and both\ntypes of failure. Explain source versus export, file versus environment, and\nwhy a restart changes this program\'s settings. For paced discussion, pause\nafter each numbered section and explain the observation before continuing.\n\nStop each watched child with Ctrl+C and observe stopped. Preserve the evidence\nbefore removing only the exact disposable directory with `rm -rf -- "$lab"`.\nThe native source gate checks the unfinished starter, working reference and an\nindependent completion. Site consent, saved attempts and exact exports are\nseparate acceptance requirements.\n',
	"LS-10-unit-4-editing-and-configuration-supplemental-3":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the accepted guide's literal shell variables.
		'# Optional extension: previewed replacements and complete file recovery\n\nComplete the required configuration-edit core first. This separate optional\nextension practices a bounded batch edit. It does not repeat the core\'s live\nreload exercise or the environment-file transfer. Its outcome is a previewed\nchange to exactly two named files, complete retained backups, validation before\napplication and exact recovery after a partial application.\n\n## Setup and the deliberately small configuration format\n\nUse an owned Linux terminal with Bash, Python 3.9 or later, GNU coreutils, sed\nwith -E support and diff. No system configuration, listener, service manager or\nroot account is needed. Start in `starter`; review `solution` after completing\nan attempt. Each role contains task.sh, validate_config.py, primary.conf,\nsecondary.conf and replacement-notes.md.\n\nThis lab\'s literal key/value format has exactly one name, note and timeout.\nAn equals sign separates key from value, surrounding whitespace is ignored,\nand # starts a comment. Blank lines and comment lines are allowed. Name and\nnote contain 1 through 80 characters. Timeout is an integer from 1 through 60\nwithout leading zeros. Unknown, missing or duplicate keys are rejected. The\nformat does not evaluate shell assignments, quoting or variable expansions;\nthe .conf files are never sourced. The supplied Python validator reads that\nformat and checks a prepared file\'s timeout against the requested value.\n\nThe fixtures deliberately include numbers in names, notes and comments. A\nbroad replacement such as replacing every 10 would corrupt unrelated text.\nThe assignment is one anchored timeout setting, preserving its indentation,\nspacing, inline comment and every unrelated byte.\n\n## 1. Inspect and initialize exactly two files\n\n~~~sh\ncat primary.conf secondary.conf\npython3 validate_config.py primary.conf\npython3 validate_config.py secondary.conf\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/unit4-replace.XXXXXX")\nbash task.sh init "$lab"\nprintf \'LAB=%s\\n\' "$lab"\ncp -p -- "$lab/primary.conf" "$lab/primary.evidence.conf"\ncp -p -- "$lab/secondary.conf" "$lab/secondary.evidence.conf"\n~~~\n\nPrimary starts with timeout 10 and secondary with timeout 20. Initialization\nrequires an empty owned directory and writes a lab marker. The controller uses\nan explicit two-file list; it does not search /etc, follow symbolic links or\nedit every file matching a glob. Quoted arguments keep paths with spaces intact.\nThe evidence copies are independent of the controller\'s later backups.\n\n## 2. Complete the named replacement\n\nRun `bash task.sh preview "$lab" 25` before editing the script. The untouched\nstarter reports `unfinished: implement the anchored timeout replacement`, exits\n78 and leaves both current files unchanged. It cleans its temporary staging\ndirectory and creates no backup. This is the named incomplete learner task.\n\nUse nano or vim to replace only the block between REPLACE_BEGIN and REPLACE_END\nin prepare_one. Its arguments are source, destination and new_value. Write a\nsed -E substitution that matches a whole timeout setting line, captures the\nprefix including whitespace, replaces only the digit sequence and retains the\nsuffix containing whitespace and an optional comment. Redirect to the supplied\ndestination, never to the source path. Redirection is opened before sed reads\ninput, so using the same filename on both sides can truncate the original.\n\nUseful pattern parts are `^` and `$` for line boundaries,\n`[[:blank:]]*` for spaces or tabs, `[0-9]+` for one or more digits, parentheses\nfor captured groups, and `(#.*)?` for an optional comment. In the replacement,\n`\\1` and `\\2` reuse the captured prefix and suffix. The controller restricts\nnew_value to decimal integers in the allowed range before constructing the\nexpression. `--` ends command options before a filename. Run `bash -n task.sh`\nafter editing to check shell syntax.\n\nThe shell interprets the expression before sed receives it. A delimiter such\nas | avoids combining the end-of-line $ anchor with @ into Bash\'s $@ argument\nexpansion; another delimiter requires appropriate shell quoting.\n\nThe supplied validator rejects missing or duplicate timeout keys before\npreparation and then verifies the requested value in the staged result. A\nreplacement that silently changes no setting is not accepted as success.\n\n## 3. Preview before applying\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh preview "$lab" 25\ncmp "$lab/primary.conf" "$lab/primary.evidence.conf"\ncmp "$lab/secondary.conf" "$lab/secondary.evidence.conf"\n~~~\n\nPredict both diff hunks before running preview. The intended diff changes only the timeout digits\nto 25; names, notes and comments must stay byte-for-byte unchanged. Both\ncmp commands exit 0 because preview leaves current files unchanged. Diff exits\n1 when differences exist, so the controller distinguishes that ordinary result\nfrom a diff error. A successful preview exits 0 after validating both prepared\nfiles. Its private staging files are removed on exit.\n\nRead the supplied orchestration around the learner function. It uses a private\nmktemp staging directory, validates all current and prepared files before\napplication, preserves current modes with chmod --reference and registers an\nEXIT trap to remove only that staging directory. `set -euo pipefail` handles\ncommand failures; it is not a substitute for the format/value validator.\n\n## 4. Apply and retain the complete backup identity\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh apply "$lab" 25\n~~~\n\nRecord the exact `backup=...` path printed by this command. Set backup to that\npath before the comparison and rollback commands; do not leave example text:\n\n~~~sh\nbackup=\'/exact/backup/path/printed/by/the/apply/command\'\ncmp "$backup/primary.conf" "$lab/primary.evidence.conf"\ncmp "$backup/secondary.conf" "$lab/secondary.evidence.conf"\npython3 validate_config.py "$lab/primary.conf" --expect-timeout 25\npython3 validate_config.py "$lab/secondary.conf" --expect-timeout 25\n~~~\n\nThe controller copies both original files and their modes into a new backup\ndirectory, writes its complete marker only after both copies succeed, then\nprints the backup identity before replacement. Each rename replaces one file\natomically. The pair is not an atomic multi-file transaction: interruption or\na failed second rename can leave the first file changed and the second old.\nThat is why the complete backup is retained separately from temporary staging.\nDo not infer an all-or-nothing batch from two successful validation commands.\n\n## 5. Reject a faulty batch and restore both files\n\nCopy the current files as fresh evidence. In only the disposable secondary\nfile, remove timeout or add a second timeout line. Predict the validation error\nand run `bash task.sh apply "$lab" 30`. It must fail before replacing either\ncurrent file or creating a new complete backup. Compare the valid primary to\nits fresh evidence and the deliberately edited secondary to its own faulty\nevidence. Repair the fault or restore it from the complete backup before the\nnext successful batch. A failed preview also must leave current bytes unchanged.\n\nTo recover the retained original pair, including after a partially applied\nbatch, run:\n\n~~~sh\nbash task.sh rollback "$lab" "$backup"\ncmp "$lab/primary.conf" "$lab/primary.evidence.conf"\ncmp "$lab/secondary.conf" "$lab/secondary.evidence.conf"\n~~~\n\nBoth comparisons must exit 0. Rollback accepts only a complete owned backup\ndirectly inside this lab with the expected marker and two regular owned files.\nIt validates both backup files before replacing either current file. An invalid\nbackup must not silently overwrite a working configuration. The native gate\nalso forces the second rename to fail, observes the partial pair, and verifies\nexplicit rollback of both retained originals. It does not claim resilience to\narbitrary machine failure during a multi-file operation.\n\n## 6. Changed cases and evidence\n\nRepeat preview/apply/rollback with requested timeout 60, then 1. Try 0, 61 or\n`2x`; the controller must reject them before editing. Change indentation and\nan inline comment in an owned file and predict which bytes the anchored edit\npreserves. Confirm that a commented example, a name containing 10 and the note\ncontaining 10 and 20 stay unchanged. Replace one owned file with a symbolic\nlink to another disposable evidence file and confirm refusal without changing\nthat target. Restore a regular owned file afterward.\n\nRecord predictions, exact diffs, exit statuses, modes, complete backup paths,\nunchanged-source comparisons, any observed partial state and exact recovery in\nreplacement-notes.md. Explain how an anchor differs from a broad text search\nand why validation precedes application. For paced discussion, pause after\neach numbered section; the same sequence supports independent work.\n\nPreserve the evidence and then remove only the exact disposable lab directory\ncreated above with `rm -rf -- "$lab"`. This task starts no persistent process.\nNative source acceptance covers the unfinished starter, reference, independent\ncompletion, actual replacement failure and cleanup. Site import/save/review and\nexact ZIP exports remain a separate acceptance gate.\n',
	"LS-08-unit-5-processes-and-job-control":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the accepted guide's literal shell variables.
		'# Core project: observe processes, signals and interactive shell jobs\n\nOperate one disposable program, predict each transition, and retain the actual\nprocess and terminal evidence. This is the required Unit 5 process-control\nproject. Its troubleshooting notebook continues this same attempt.\n\n## Equipment and the learner task\n\nUse an owned Linux VM, WSL distribution or Linux terminal with Python 3.9 or\nlater, Bash with job control, procps `ps`, grep and coreutils (including mktemp, sleep and tee). Check `uname -s`, `id`,\n`python3 --version` and `bash --version`. The interactive part requires a real\nterminal or pseudoterminal; a web editor, redirected script or CI command\nwithout a controlling terminal cannot demonstrate `fg` and `bg`.\n\nBoth roles supply the same working process_worker.py. It is lab equipment,\nnot an additional Python implementation assignment. The required unfinished\nstarter work is the named `process-evidence` task in process-notes.md: record\npredictions, actual identities, observed states, raw output, exit statuses,\nchanged cases and cleanup, then explain the transitions. Reference notes give\nthe reasoning, not invented machine-specific IDs. Running working equipment\ndoes not complete the observation record.\n\nThe program reports real PID, parent PID, numeric UID, process group and\nsession. It accepts --lifetime from 0.2 through 1800 seconds, default 600.\nUSR1 requests a snapshot; TERM normally records a graceful stop and exits 0;\nINT records a stop and exits 130. HUP with an open output stream exits 129.\n--ignore-term records TERM but keeps running for the controlled escalation\ncase. KILL cannot be caught and produces no final program record. An autonomous\ndeadline bounds a forgotten running process; it does not run while a process\nis stopped. After continuation, elapsed wall time still counts toward it.\n\n## 1. Launch and inspect a child\n\nBegin in the selected role directory. Run commands individually in the same\nBash shell and keep it open so it can wait for its own child.\n\n~~~bash\nrole_dir=$(pwd -P)\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/unit5-process.XXXXXX")\nprintf \'EVIDENCE=%s\\nSHELL_PID=%s\\n\' "$lab" "$$"\npython3 -u "$role_dir/process_worker.py" --lifetime 600 >"$lab/cooperative.jsonl" 2>&1 &\npid=$!\nprintf \'CHILD_PID=%s\\n\' "$pid"\n~~~\n\n`&` starts an asynchronous command. `$!` captures that command\'s PID, not a\njob number or the shell\'s `$$`. `-u` and the program\'s flush keep output\nobservable after redirection. `>` writes the file and `2>&1` directs errors\nto the same destination. A launch is not proof that initialization succeeded.\nWait for the started record, with a bounded readiness check:\n\n~~~bash\nready=0\nfor attempt in {1..50}; do\n    if grep -q \'"event":"started"\' "$lab/cooperative.jsonl"; then\n        ready=1\n        break\n    fi\n    sleep 0.1\ndone\nprintf \'READY=%s\\n\' "$ready"\ncat "$lab/cooperative.jsonl"\nps -p "$pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,pgid,sid,stat,args | tee "$lab/ps-running.txt"\njobs -l\n~~~\n\nContinue only with READY=1 and matching live PID, UID and command. The direct\nchild\'s PPID matches this Bash shell. A sleeping S state is ordinary while the\nworker waits; state alone does not prove correctness. Optional `top -b -n 1 -p\n"$pid"` gives a resource snapshot when procps top is installed. PID, process\ngroup, session and job number describe different relationships. The shell\'s\njobs table contains its own jobs rather than every process on the machine.\n\nA PID identifies one process; its PPID identifies its current parent. UID is the\naccount identity used for ownership and permission checks. A process group\ncollects related processes so signals can address the group together; a shell\npipeline can have several processes in one job. A session contains process\ngroups and can have a controlling terminal. That terminal designates one group\nas foreground. PGID and SID identify those two groupings; they are not new job\nnumbers. Starting a nested Bash does not automatically create a new session.\nThis one-command job often has its worker PID as PGID, but inspect the actual\nvalues instead of assuming all identifiers are interchangeable. Elapsed values\nin the JSON records are wall-clock seconds since this worker started.\n\n## 2. Signal, observe and reap\n\n~~~bash\nkill -USR1 "$pid"\nsleep 0.2\ncat "$lab/cooperative.jsonl"\nkill -TERM "$pid"\nif wait "$pid"; then status=0; else status=$?; fi\nprintf \'WAIT_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$status" | tee "$lab/wait-status.txt"\ncat "$lab/cooperative.jsonl"\nps -p "$pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,stat,args\n~~~\n\nPredict a snapshot from the same PID, followed by a TERM stopped record and\nWAIT_STATUS=0. `wait` retrieves this child result and reaps its status; it is\nnot a timer. The `if` keeps a nonzero result observable even in a shell using\nerrexit. The final ps has no row for this ended child and returns 1. That is\nthe expected absence result. If the deadline already ended the worker, retain\nthat evidence and repeat in a fresh lab instead of signaling an old PID.\n\nA terminated child can remain Z until its parent reaps it. Bash can reap jobs\nasynchronously, so a fleeting or absent Z row does not disprove that mechanism.\nAn orphan has lost its original parent; its new PPID depends on namespace and\nsubreaper policy and is not invariably 1. Neither state means a program is\nstill doing useful work. Service supervision is a later unit.\n\n## 3. A deliberate refusal and a changed case\n\nLaunch another worker into a new log in this owned directory:\n\n~~~bash\npython3 -u "$role_dir/process_worker.py" --ignore-term --lifetime 600 >"$lab/stubborn.jsonl" 2>&1 &\npid=$!\n~~~\n\nRepeat the bounded readiness check with stubborn.jsonl. Confirm this new PID,\nowner and command with ps, then send TERM. Wait 0.2 seconds and inspect the log:\nan ignored record and the same live PID demonstrate the refusal. Confirm that\nidentity again immediately before `kill -KILL "$pid"`. Use the same conditional\nwait pattern and retain WAIT_STATUS=137. The operating system killed it; there\nis no stopped record from the program. A zero result from `kill -0` would only\nshow existence/permission, not useful progress or graceful shutdown.\n\nFor a changed case, run `python3 process_worker.py --lifetime 0.5` in the\nforeground. Record its actual identity, a deadline stop and status 0. Run\n`python3 process_worker.py --lifetime nan` and record the distinct argument\ndiagnostic/status 2 with no started record. Do not turn either failure or\ndeadline text into a claim that a requested signal was handled.\n\n## 4. Use actual terminal job control\n\nOpen a fresh interactive Bash inside the role directory:\n\n~~~bash\nbash --noprofile --norc -i\njobs -l\nset -o | grep monitor\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/unit5-terminal.XXXXXX")\nprintf \'TERMINAL_EVIDENCE=%s\\nSHELL_PID=%s\\n\' "$lab" "$$"\npython3 -u process_worker.py --lifetime 600 >"$lab/interactive.jsonl" 2>&1\n~~~\n\nThe jobs table starts empty and monitor is on. The last command now owns the\nforeground. Press **Ctrl+Z**, then run `jobs -l`. It reports a stopped job and\nits actual PID. In this fresh shell that job is %1; use the displayed jobspec\nif a different number appears. Assign pid to the exact displayed PID and use\nps to record its T state, PGID and session. A stop retains the process and its\nidentity; it is different from exit and cannot be reaped as completed work.\n\n~~~bash\nbg %1\njobs -l\nkill -USR1 %1\nsleep 0.2\ncat "$lab/interactive.jsonl"\nfg %1\n~~~\n\n`bg` continues the job without giving it the terminal foreground. The continued\nand snapshot records have the same PID as before the stop. The Bash builtin\nkill understands its own jobspec; an external command does not share that jobs\ntable. The worker does not read from the terminal, so it can continue safely\nin the background. A background terminal reader can instead stop with TTIN.\n\nAfter fg, press **Ctrl+C**, then immediately run:\n\n~~~bash\nprintf \'FOREGROUND_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"\njobs -l\ncat "$lab/interactive.jsonl"\n~~~\n\nThe terminal sends INT to the foreground process group. This worker catches\nit and exits 130; fg returns that result. The shell regains the foreground and\nthe job disappears. INT is catchable; KILL and STOP are not. Ctrl+Z uses TSTP,\nwhich programs can handle, while this worker leaves its default stop behavior.\nA script started without a terminal cannot demonstrate these terminal transitions. Record the actual terminal sequence and results, not a printed\nprediction of it.\n\n## 5. Explain, retain and clean up\n\nComplete every process-notes.md item with actual observations. Explain the\ndifference between `$!`, `$$` and %1; PID/PPID/UID; S, T and Z; graceful exit,\nignored TERM, KILL, waiting, and foreground process groups. Compare the\nreference reasoning after producing an attempt. Pause after each numbered\nsection for paced discussion, or follow the same sequence independently.\n\nIn each shell, inspect jobs and ps for the exact recorded identities, stop and\nwait for any remaining owned worker, and confirm it is gone. Resume a stopped\njob before requesting a graceful stop. Never target process names or PIDs that\nthe lab did not create. Preserve logs and notes, then remove only the exact\nprinted disposable evidence directory. `exit` leaves the fresh inner Bash;\nretain and clean the outer shell\'s separate evidence directory as well.\n\nReferences: [Bash job control](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Job-Control-Basics.html),\n[job-control builtins](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Job-Control-Builtins.html),\n[Linux process state](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc_pid_stat.5.html).\n',
	"LS-11-unit-5-processes-and-job-control-supplemental-2":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the accepted guide's literal shell variables.
		'# Optional transfer: one pipeline job, two processes and several statuses\n\nComplete the required process-control core first. This separate transfer asks\nhow a job containing two processes differs from the core\'s single command.\nObserve actual data flow, group-wide signals and failure propagation. Keep a\nseparate pipeline-notes.md attempt; the core notebook remains with the core.\n\n## Supplied equipment and unfinished observation work\n\nUse an owned Linux terminal, Python 3.9 or later, Bash, procps ps, grep and GNU\ncoreutils. Job control requires a controlling terminal. Both roles supply the\nsame complete pipeline_worker.py. The required unfinished work is the named\npipeline-evidence record in pipeline-notes.md. Reference notes explain the\nrelationships but do not supply fabricated local PIDs or observations.\n\nThe program has produce and consume modes. Produce writes JSON items to stdout;\nconsume reads those items from its stdin pipe and writes received records to\nstdout. Both write diagnostic JSON to stderr, including real PID/PPID/UID,\nPGID and SID. Redirecting diagnostic stderr separately keeps it out of the data\nprotocol. The at field is Unix wall-clock seconds for correlating records; it\nis not CPU time. Consume refuses terminal input rather than silently reading\ncommands from the interactive shell.\n\n--lifetime accepts 0.2 through 1800 seconds, default 600. Producer --count N\nends after N items; 0 means run until a signal or deadline. --exit-code chooses\nits normal count-completion status from 0 through 125. Consumer --take N ends\nafter N items; 0 reads until EOF, a signal or deadline. Counts are 0 through\n1000. Mode-inappropriate options and invalid values are rejected with status 2.\n\nUSR1 requests a diagnostic snapshot and CONT records a continuation. TERM\nexits 0; INT exits 130. Produce restores the usual Unix SIGPIPE disposition:\nwriting after the pipe loses its reader can terminate it with SIGPIPE. That\nsignal cannot produce a final record from this program. A finite deadline is\na fallback, not evidence that a requested signal worked.\n\n## 1. Start a fresh interactive job\n\nFrom the selected role directory, start a fresh shell and confirm an empty\njobs table and monitor on. Unset prompt commands so status inspection has no\nextra commands between the experiment and its diagnostic.\n\n~~~bash\nbash --noprofile --norc -i\nunset PROMPT_COMMAND\njobs -l\nset -o | grep monitor\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/unit5-pipeline.XXXXXX")\nprintf \'PIPELINE_EVIDENCE=%s\\n\' "$lab"\npython3 -u pipeline_worker.py produce --lifetime 600 2>"$lab/producer.jsonl" |\n    python3 -u pipeline_worker.py consume --lifetime 600 >"$lab/received.jsonl" 2>"$lab/consumer.jsonl" &\nlast_pid=$!\nprintf \'LAST_PID=%s\\n\' "$last_pid"\n~~~\n\nThe pipe connects producer stdout to consumer stdin. The final & backgrounds\nthe whole pipeline. In this fresh shell it is job %1. `$!` identifies its last\nprocess, the consumer; it does not identify every process in the job. Confirm\nboth started records before interpreting a launch as readiness:\n\n~~~bash\nready=0\nfor attempt in {1..50}; do\n    if grep -q \'"event":"started"\' "$lab/producer.jsonl" &&\n       grep -q \'"event":"started"\' "$lab/consumer.jsonl"; then\n        ready=1\n        break\n    fi\n    sleep 0.1\ndone\nprintf \'READY=%s\\n\' "$ready"\ncat "$lab/producer.jsonl" "$lab/consumer.jsonl"\nproducer_pid=$(python3 -c \'import json,sys; print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).readline())["pid"])\' "$lab/producer.jsonl")\nps -p "$producer_pid,$last_pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,pgid,sid,stat,args\njobs -l\njobs -p\nhead -n 3 "$lab/received.jsonl"\n~~~\n\nContinue only with READY=1 and matching live identities/commands. The two PIDs\ndiffer, share a process group, and belong to this shell\'s job. In this Bash\nsetup the producer leads that group, so jobs -p and the shared PGID differ\nfrom the consumer\'s `$!`. The received records identify the actual producer and\nconsumer rather than a canned message. A job number is local to this shell;\na process group is the kernel grouping used for group signal delivery.\n\n## 2. Stop and resume both members\n\n~~~bash\nkill -STOP %1\njobs -l\nps -p "$producer_pid,$last_pid" -o pid,ppid,uid,pgid,sid,stat,args\nbg %1\nkill -USR1 %1\nsleep 0.2\ncat "$lab/producer.jsonl" "$lab/consumer.jsonl"\ntail -n 3 "$lab/received.jsonl"\n~~~\n\nPredict T for both members after STOP. The Bash builtin understands %1 and\nsignals the job\'s process group; a generic external command has no access to\nthis shell\'s job table. After bg, the same two identities continue, both record\nCONT/snapshot events, and new data arrives. Compare sequence numbers before\nand after, not just existence. Neither worker reads the terminal in this job.\n\nRun `fg %1`, wait until it owns the foreground, and press Ctrl+C. Immediately\nprint `printf \'FOREGROUND_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"`. The foreground group receives\nINT, the workers record their stops, and fg returns 130. Confirm jobs is empty\nand neither recorded process remains. Record actual outcomes if a deadline or\nanother event happened first; repeat with fresh evidence rather than signaling\nold PIDs.\n\n## 3. Preserve component status before another command overwrites it\n\nThe next pipelines are bounded and run in the foreground. Before each, predict\nthe producer status, consumer status and overall pipeline status.\n\n~~~bash\nset +o pipefail\npython3 -u pipeline_worker.py produce --count 3 --exit-code 7 2>"$lab/fail-default-producer.jsonl" |\n    python3 -u pipeline_worker.py consume >"$lab/fail-default-received.jsonl" 2>"$lab/fail-default-consumer.jsonl"\nprintf \'PIPELINE_STATUS=%s COMPONENTS=%s\\n\' "$?" "${PIPESTATUS[*]}"\nset -o pipefail\npython3 -u pipeline_worker.py produce --count 3 --exit-code 7 2>"$lab/fail-checked-producer.jsonl" |\n    python3 -u pipeline_worker.py consume >"$lab/fail-checked-received.jsonl" 2>"$lab/fail-checked-consumer.jsonl"\nprintf \'PIPELINE_STATUS=%s COMPONENTS=%s\\n\' "$?" "${PIPESTATUS[*]}"\n~~~\n\nBoth component lists are 7 0. The producer completed three items and returned\nthe requested 7; the consumer received them, observed EOF and returned 0. By\ndefault Bash reports the last command\'s 0. With pipefail it reports the\nrightmost nonzero component, here 7. EOF is a pipe condition, not a guarantee\nthat upstream succeeded. Both parameter expansions happen before printf runs;\nrunning cat or another command first would replace the status information.\nRetain the printed diagnostics and actual JSON files.\n\n## 4. A reader that deliberately ends early\n\n~~~bash\nset +o pipefail\npython3 -u pipeline_worker.py produce --lifetime 5 2>"$lab/early-producer.jsonl" |\n    python3 -u pipeline_worker.py consume --take 1 --lifetime 5 >"$lab/early-received.jsonl" 2>"$lab/early-consumer.jsonl"\nprintf \'PIPELINE_STATUS=%s COMPONENTS=%s\\n\' "$?" "${PIPESTATUS[*]}"\nset -o pipefail\npython3 -u pipeline_worker.py produce --lifetime 5 2>"$lab/early-checked-producer.jsonl" |\n    python3 -u pipeline_worker.py consume --take 1 --lifetime 5 >"$lab/early-checked-received.jsonl" 2>"$lab/early-checked-consumer.jsonl"\nprintf \'PIPELINE_STATUS=%s COMPONENTS=%s\\n\' "$?" "${PIPESTATUS[*]}"\n~~~\n\nThe consumer intentionally accepts one item and exits 0. The next write reaches\na closed pipe and terminates this producer with SIGPIPE. Bash reports its\ncomponent as 141 (128 + 13). Expect overall 0 without pipefail and 141 with it,\nwhile both component lists remain 141 0. Inspect the actual early-reader and\nproducer records. Pipefail changes reporting, not the reason the consumer\nstopped, and a nonzero upstream result does not by itself explain intent.\n\n## 5. Explain and clean up\n\nComplete pipeline-notes.md with predictions, both identities and group/session\nrelationships, stopped/continued states, data sequences, all four component\nstatus comparisons and the actual signal/EOF records. Explain how a group-wide\naction differs from signaling only the PID in `$!`. Diagnose one mismatch and\nretain its correction. The reference supplies reasoning after an attempt.\nPause after each section for paced discussion or use the same sequence alone.\n\nInspect jobs and ps for the exact owned identities. Stop/reap any remaining\nowned job before cleanup; resume a stopped job before graceful termination.\nPreserve the evidence, remove only this printed temporary directory, then exit\nthe fresh inner shell. Restore the original shell\'s pipefail preference when\nusing an existing shell instead. This transfer does not change system services.\n\nReferences: [Bash pipelines](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Pipelines.html),\n[job-control builtins](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Job-Control-Builtins.html).\n',
	"LS-12-unit-5-processes-and-job-control-supplemental-3":
		// eslint-disable-next-line no-template-curly-in-string -- Preserve the accepted guide's literal shell variables.
		'# Optional extension: zombies, waiting and an observed adopter\n\nComplete the process-control core first. This separate extension observes what\nhappens after exit or loss of a parent. It does not repeat the core\'s signal\nexercise or the pipeline transfer. Keep a separate adoption-notes.md attempt.\n\n## Equipment, boundaries and unfinished observation work\n\nUse an owned Linux VM, WSL distribution or Linux terminal with /proc, Python\n3.9 or later, the standard C library and procps ps. No root, service-manager\nchange, new namespace or system-wide setting is needed. The supplied program\nenables and reads back the child-subreaper attribute on **its own new process**.\nIts descendants remain inside this disposable experiment.\n\nBoth roles supply the same complete adoption_lab.py. The required unfinished\nwork is the named adoption-evidence record in adoption-notes.md. Reference\nnotes explain the relationships without pretending to contain this machine\'s\nPIDs or raw observations. Equipment success does not complete the record.\n\nThe at field is Unix wall-clock seconds. A child startTicks value is its Linux\nstart time in clock ticks since boot; compare it with PID to recognize the same\nbirth identity, rather than confusing it with a wall-clock timestamp or status.\n\nPID names a process, PPID names its current parent, and UID is its account\nidentity. A zombie has exited but its parent has not yet collected the result.\nIt cannot do more work or handle signals. An orphan has lost its original\nparent. On Linux it can be adopted by the nearest living ancestor marked as a\nsubreaper; otherwise namespace/init policy determines the adopter. Do not assume\nthat every new PPID is 1. These are different state and relationship questions.\n\nThis experiment explicitly makes its observer a subreaper, confirms that flag,\nand later verifies the actual adopter through /proc. Child processes do not\ninherit that flag. The demonstration changes this one process\'s role rather\nthan silently changing how the whole machine supervises processes.\n\n## 1. Read and predict the two stages\n\nThe first child exits 7 immediately. The observer deliberately leaves it\nunreaped during an observation window, confirms real Z state and its actual\nparent/UID, then waits for that exact child and records 7.\n\nThe second stage creates an original parent and its child. Pipes keep the\noriginal parent alive until its child\'s initial identity has been observed.\nOnly then does the observer release that parent and wait for its exit 0. The\ngrandchild remains alive, loses its original parent, and is adopted by the\nexplicit subreaper. Its PID, birth time and UID remain unchanged while PPID\nchanges to the observer. After a second observation window, another pipe release\nlets the adopted child exit 9. The observer can now wait for that adopted child.\n\n--hold-seconds accepts 0.1 through 120, default 5, for each observation window.\nThe children have separate bounded lifetimes and close unused pipe ends. Normal\ncompletion and a handled interruption clean up and reap the owned descendants.\nSIGKILL cannot run a process\'s cleanup code; use ordinary Ctrl+C/TERM when\nexamining this program\'s interruption path.\n\n## 2. Start in terminal A and retain the actual identities\n\nFrom the selected role folder:\n\n~~~bash\nuname -s\nid\npython3 --version\nlab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/unit5-adoption.XXXXXX")\nprintf \'ADOPTION_EVIDENCE=%s\\n\' "$lab"\npython3 -u adoption_lab.py --hold-seconds 60 --record "$lab/observations.jsonl"\n~~~\n\nKeep terminal A running. The first started record identifies the observer,\nits actual UID and the observed subreaper flag. The spawned and zombie_observed\nrecords give the first child PID and actual Z state. In terminal B, use Bash\nand enter the exact evidence directory printed by terminal A. The following\nassignments read the observer and first child from that run\'s saved JSON lines:\n\n~~~bash\nread -r -p \'Evidence directory printed by terminal A: \' lab\nobserver=$(python3 -c \'import json,sys; print(next(r["pid"] for r in map(json.loads,open(sys.argv[1])) if r["event"]=="started"))\' "$lab/observations.jsonl")\nchild=$(python3 -c \'import json,sys; print(next(r["child"]["pid"] for r in map(json.loads,open(sys.argv[1])) if r["event"]=="zombie_observed"))\' "$lab/observations.jsonl")\nps -p "$observer,$child" -o pid,ppid,uid,pgid,sid,stat,args\n~~~\n\nPredict the first child\'s PPID and Z state, then compare with ps and the JSON\nrecord. Retain the real ps output. The observation window is finite. If it\nhas ended before inspection, keep the record and rerun in a fresh evidence\ndirectory rather than inspecting a possibly reused old PID.\n\n## 3. Observe waiting and actual adoption\n\nAfter the first window, a reaped record reports the first child\'s status 7.\nThe next spawned and grandchild_observed records identify the original parent\nand its child before reparenting. The program uses a pipe handshake so the\noriginal parent cannot exit before that initial relationship is observed.\n\nAn adopted record then reports the same grandchild birth identity and UID, its\nactual new PPID, and the original parent\'s PID. During the second window,\nread the adopted child\'s actual PID in terminal B, then inspect it:\n\n~~~bash\nchild=$(python3 -c \'import json,sys; print(next(r["child"]["pid"] for r in map(json.loads,open(sys.argv[1])) if r["event"]=="adopted"))\' "$lab/observations.jsonl")\nps -p "$observer,$child" -o pid,ppid,uid,pgid,sid,stat,args\n~~~\n\nCompare\nthe actual new PPID with the observer\'s PID. The original parent has already\nbeen waited for with status 0. The grandchild is alive while waiting on its\nrelease pipe; its ordinary sleeping state is different from the earlier Z.\n\nAfter the second window, the observer releases and waits for the adopted child,\nrecords status 9 and reports cleanup with no owned children and closed pipe\ndescriptors. Back in terminal A, immediately retain:\n\n~~~bash\nprintf \'PROGRAM_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"\n~~~\n\nNormal program status is 0. The internal child statuses 7, 0 and 9 are\nobservations, not an automatic failure of the overall experiment. --record\nwrites the same events to a new evidence file and refuses an existing path\nbefore spawning children, with diagnostic status 2. To check this protection,\nrepeat the same command with the same --record path after a completed run;\nconfirm that it refuses and leaves the saved bytes intact. Use a fresh path\nfor each further experiment. Keep the displayed output and recorded events.\n\n## 4. Change a case and distinguish an interruption\n\nRepeat with --hold-seconds 0.5 and fresh evidence. The shorter observation\nwindows do not change which child exits 7, which parent exits 0, or which\nadopted child exits 9. Record the new actual IDs and timing instead of copying\nthe first run. Run `python3 adoption_lab.py --hold-seconds nan` and retain its\nargument diagnostic/status 2; no started record is produced.\n\nFor a separate interrupted run, use a new evidence filename:\n\n~~~bash\npython3 -u adoption_lab.py --hold-seconds 60 --record "$lab/interrupted.jsonl"\n~~~\n\nAfter zombie_observed appears, press Ctrl+C. Immediately print the program\'s\nstatus. The observer records interruption and cleanup, returns 130, and reaps\nits exited child. This is not a completed two-stage adoption case; retain a\nnormal run separately. A display pipe can close before final output, so the\nexplicit record file retains evidence independently of that display stream.\nThe native gate also interrupts after adoption to check that remaining owned\ndescendants are cleaned in that phase.\n\nIf /proc or the subreaper operation is unavailable, retain the diagnostic and\nuse an owned supported Linux environment. Do not replace actual reparenting\nwith a predicted diagram or treat a different platform as Linux acceptance.\n\n## 5. Explain, retain and clean up\n\nComplete adoption-notes.md with predictions, raw event and ps records, UID/PID\nand parent identities, the two Z/live observations, all wait results, the\nsubreaper flag, the actual new parent, the changed run and interruption. Explain\nwhy KILL cannot remove an already exited zombie and why the parent/adopter must\ncollect its result. Identify the evidence that excludes a race where the\noriginal parent disappeared before its child was first observed.\n\nThe same numbered sequence supports independent reading or pauses for paced\ndiscussion. Compare the reference reasoning after an attempt. Confirm that the\nobserved processes are gone, retain the evidence, then remove only each exact\nprinted disposable directory. The program\'s owned child cleanup does not remove\nthe saved evidence directory. Record any failure and correction before treating\nthe experiment as complete.\n\nReferences: [waiting and zombie state](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/waitpid.2.html),\n[the child-subreaper attribute](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER.2const.html).\n',
	"LS-10-unit-9-package-management-and-software-layout":
		"# Installed Packages, Command Ownership and File Layout\n\nThis is the required Unit 9 Linux Systems project. Investigate an installed\npackage and connect its database records to actual files. Complete the core\nbefore choosing the optional manual-install comparison or dependency-recovery\nlab. The troubleshooting notebook continues this investigation; it is not a\nsecond copy of the project.\n\n## Environment and saved work\n\nUse an ordinary account in a Debian or Ubuntu Linux lab, including a suitable\nWSL distribution. Python 3, Bash, `dpkg-query` and an installed `bash` package\nare needed. The collector only reads installed metadata and file information;\nit writes the report path supplied on the command line. It rejects root,\nunsupported platforms, missing packages, invalid query names and existing\nreport files. It neither installs nor upgrades packages. Keep the original\nreports alongside the notes so a later run cannot erase the first observation.\n\nThe site's IDE edits and saves these sources and notes. After confirming the\ncourse import, open `package-notes.md` first, download the ZIP and extract it.\nRun the commands in that extracted role folder in the Linux terminal. Saving\nin the browser does not execute Linux commands. Save observations back into\nthe same attempt; the reference is a comparison after an independent attempt.\n\n## What a package manager tracks\n\nAn installed package is a versioned collection of files plus metadata. Its\ndatabase can identify the package version, architecture, dependencies, state\nand recorded paths. One package can place commands, documentation, support\nfiles and configuration in different directories. A directory name alone does\nnot establish who installed a file. Query the database and inspect the actual\npath together.\n\nDebian-family systems commonly use APT to select repository versions and\nresolve dependency changes; dpkg maintains installed-package state. RPM-family\nsystems commonly use DNF and an RPM database. Older systems can expose `yum`;\nthe distribution's version and documentation determine its behavior. These\nfamilies share questions but not interchangeable commands or databases. This\nproject's executable collector is explicitly for the Debian family.\n\nDependencies describe relationships between packages, not a complete runtime\ntrace. A `Depends` entry can contain version constraints or alternatives. Do\nnot split it into a list of commands to install. An installed database record\nalso does not prove the original download's authenticity or repository.\n\n## Prediction and first observation\n\nIn `package-notes.md`, predict whether Bash's command, manual, startup\nconfiguration and support files will all occupy one folder. Predict whether\nthe package database will name the same command path that the shell resolves.\nThen run:\n\n```sh\nuname -s\nid -u\npython3 --version\ndpkg-query --version\nbash task.sh --package bash --command bash --report bash-first.json\npython3 -m json.tool bash-first.json\n```\n\nExpect a successful collection with an installed package version and a list\nof paths. Exact versions and counts depend on the lab; the notes must record\nactual values. Exit status zero confirms collection, not completion of the\nlearning tasks. `learnerWorksheetCompletionInferred` remains false.\n\n`package_report.py` runs the queries with an explicit argument list, records\ntheir actual status and timing, and requires an `ok` error flag and current\nstate `installed`. The full status also includes a separate requested action:\n`hold ok installed` and `deinstall ok installed` can still describe installed\nfiles. Read the report's `state` fields separately; a requested removal is not\nproof of completed removal. No missing-input error is converted into an empty\nsuccessful count. A report is created only after the queries complete. Its\nmode is private to the current account and an existing report is not replaced.\n\n## Connect the records to files\n\nIdentify at least one recorded command and one documentation path. Inspect\nboth with `ls -ld`, quoting each path. Explain whether each is a file,\ndirectory or symbolic link and whether it exists. Find a configuration or\nservice path if this package records one; record \"not supplied by this\npackage\" when it does not. Do not invent a service just to fill a category.\n\nConventional locations provide clues: `/etc` holds system configuration;\n`/usr/bin` and related command directories hold executables; `/usr/lib` holds\nlibraries and other support data; systemd unit directories contain service\ndefinitions; `/usr/share/doc` and `/usr/share/man` contain documentation.\nThese categories are investigative aids, not a guarantee that every entry is\nan executable or that every distribution uses exactly the same layout.\n\nRead the report's `executable` section separately from its `package` section.\nThe shell searches `PATH`; the resulting command can belong to a different\npackage or to a manual install. Merged-/usr systems can expose `/bin` as a\nlink to `/usr/bin`, while the package database retains the older spelling.\nThe collector checks a legacy alias only when it names the same actual file.\nRecord each ownership query's status and result. An untracked command does\nnot become package-managed simply because it runs successfully.\n\n## Changed cases and failure evidence\n\nCollect a second installed package, for example:\n\n```sh\nbash task.sh --package coreutils --command ls --report coreutils-first.json\n```\n\nCompare the package version, dependency text and layout. The executable's\nowner is reported independently. Predict and then try an intentionally absent\npackage, using a fresh report name. Confirm a nonzero status and no completed\nreport. Retry the first successful command with `--report bash-first.json`;\nit must reject replacement and preserve the original bytes. These are normal\ndiagnostic cases, not reasons to hide an error with `|| true`.\n\n## Plan a package change without performing it\n\nWrite a change plan before any actual package modification. Name the current\nversion, desired version, reason, source, dependency changes, service/reboot\neffects, preservation of local configuration, rollback evidence, and a\npost-change command that demonstrates the required behavior. Consult local\nAPT metadata with `apt-cache policy bash`. A candidate version is a current\nselection from configured metadata, not proof of where an already installed\ncopy originally came from. Inspect the read-only\nsimulation `apt-get --simulate install bash` and explain its proposed changes.\nPackage lists may be stale or absent; record that limitation. A successful\nsimulation is not an installation and does not establish runtime health.\n\nIf an approved disposable lab later performs a real change, capture before\nand after versions, actual install status, service restart results and the\nbehavioral check. Package installation can leave a package unpacked but\nunconfigured when a dependency is missing. Keep that state distinct from an\ninstalled and healthy application. The optional dependency lab examines this\nwith a separate owned database rather than the host's installed packages.\n\n## Completion and guided walkthrough\n\nFinish the five named tasks in the notes: prediction, package/file comparison,\nchanged package, failure/record preservation, and proposed change plan. At\nleast one prediction must be compared with actual evidence. Explain one\nsurprise rather than silently rewriting the prediction. Walk through the same\nrecord in a guided walkthrough, including the exact path and version that support\neach claim. The reference notes explain a valid method and interpretation;\nthey do not supply fabricated observations for this lab.\n\nFor an RPM-family lab, use the distribution's documented `rpm -qi`, `rpm -ql`\nand `rpm -qf` queries and DNF's installed-package/version information to answer\nthe same questions. Save that as a separate observation, clearly naming the\nplatform. Do not run this Debian collector and label its failure an RPM\npackage investigation.\n\nPrimary references: [dpkg-query](https://manpages.debian.org/trixie/dpkg/dpkg-query.1.en.html),\n[dpkg](https://manpages.debian.org/trixie/dpkg/dpkg.1.en.html) and\n[Debian package metadata](https://www.debian.org/doc/manuals/debian-handbook/sect.package-meta-information.en.html).\n",
	"LS-19-unit-9-package-management-and-software-layout-supplemental-2":
		'# Manual Release Layout and Package Ownership\n\nThis optional Unit 9 transfer project follows the installed-package core.\nBuild a supplied small program from source, make a release archive, install it\nunder an owned prefix, compare its ownership with the package database, and\nobserve a version switch and rollback. It is a separate attempt from the core\ninventory. The dependency-recovery extension studies a different problem.\n\n## Environment and learner task\n\nUse an ordinary account in an owned Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab with Python 3,\nBash, `cc` and `dpkg-query`. Arrange for missing tools to be available before\nthe experiment. The C source is complete equipment; implementing C functions\nis not the assignment. Both roles provide the same working driver and source.\nThe unfinished work is the five-part evidence record in `manual-notes.md`.\nThe reference supplies interpretation, not invented results from this machine.\n\nAfter confirming the site IDE import, open `manual-notes.md`, save/download\nthe ZIP and extract the selected role. Run the following commands in that\nrole\'s Linux terminal. Browser editing does not compile or install Linux code.\nBring the observations back to the same saved attempt.\n\n## Source, build, archive and install are different objects\n\nSource is the human-readable C file. A compiler turns it into an executable\nfor this lab\'s platform. The archive bundles that executable and a manifest.\nInstalling the archive copies its two files into a release-specific directory;\nan explicit link selects the active release. These steps leave different\nevidence. A successful compilation does not show installation, and a recorded\nhash does not establish who supplied trustworthy source.\n\nHere the version is a compiler definition, so the same source produces 1.0\nand 2.0 binaries. The flags request C17, normal diagnostics and rejection of\nwarnings. No system package is installed. No shell startup file or global PATH\nis changed. The driver is deliberately restricted to the two supplied versions,\nand it refuses existing builds, archives and installed release directories.\nIt writes only its fresh marker-protected lab; run it without sudo.\n\n## 1. Predict, then build the first release\n\nPredict whether `dpkg-query` will own a binary merely because it was compiled\nsuccessfully. Predict what will change when a version is selected and what\nrollback will need. Keep the predictions in the notes before running:\n\n```bash\nuname -s\nid -u\npython3 --version\ncc --version\nlab_parent=$(mktemp -d)\nlab="$lab_parent/manual"\nbash task.sh init --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh build --lab "$lab" --version 1.0\nbash task.sh archive --lab "$lab" --version 1.0\n```\n\nKeep this shell open and retain the exact `OWNED_LAB` path. `init` requires a\nnew path and creates a private marker and operation log. The build appears\nunder `builds/1.0`; the archive and its recorded SHA-256 appear under `archives`.\nRead `layout_demo.c`: `--version` prints the selected version, `--status`\nprints a status line, and an unsupported argument returns status 2.\n\nInspect the build and archive as different objects:\n\n```bash\n"$lab/builds/1.0/layout-demo" --version\ntar -tzf "$lab/archives/1.0.tar.gz"\ncat "$lab/archives/1.0.sha256"\n```\n\nExpect version `1.0` and two archive files: `bin/layout-demo` and\n`share/layout-demo/manifest.json`. These lab-specific paths are chosen to make\nthe distinction visible; they are not a requirement for every upstream program.\n\n## 2. Install, select and inspect real ownership\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh install --lab "$lab" --version 1.0\nbash task.sh activate --lab "$lab" --version 1.0\nbash task.sh check --lab "$lab"\nreadlink "$lab/current"\n"$lab/current/bin/layout-demo" --status\n```\n\nThe active link names `releases/1.0`, and the runtime reports 1.0.\n`check` compares installed binary bytes with the release manifest, runs that\nbinary\'s version query and asks the real installed dpkg database about its\nexact path. In this fresh owned prefix, expect ownership status 1 and\n`packageManaged: false`. That query result is an observed ownership answer,\nnot a failure to compile or run. A different dpkg query error is a failed\ncheck and is retained as such.\n\nThe manual manifest relates this installed binary to the recorded build;\nit does not enroll it in dpkg or prove authenticity. The person operating a\nmanual install must retain source/version information, build commands,\narchive hashes, configuration placement, update responsibility and recovery\ninstructions. Compare this burden with the required core\'s package records.\n\n## 3. Change the version and demonstrate rollback\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh build --lab "$lab" --version 2.0\nbash task.sh archive --lab "$lab" --version 2.0\nbash task.sh install --lab "$lab" --version 2.0\nbash task.sh activate --lab "$lab" --version 2.0\nbash task.sh check --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh activate --lab "$lab" --version 1.0\nbash task.sh check --lab "$lab"\n```\n\nRequire actual runtime versions 2.0 and then 1.0; the link\'s name alone is\ninsufficient. Record the binary hashes and the three runtime observations.\nActivation changes the link atomically after validating the chosen release.\nThe other installed release remains present. Rollback here does not restore\na database, migrate user data, undo external effects or restart a service;\nthis supplied program has none of those responsibilities. A real change plan\nmust address them when the application has them.\n\n## 4. Preserve a failure and an outside sentinel\n\nRetry `archive` for the already recorded version 1.0. Confirm a nonzero status\nand unchanged archive bytes. Do not turn this diagnostic into success with\n`|| true`. Before testing removal, create a small sentinel file in the parent\ndirectory, outside the lab, and record its bytes. Remove release 2.0 while 1.0\nis active, then check 1.0 again:\n\n```bash\nprintf \'outside sentinel\\n\' >"$lab_parent/sentinel.txt"\nbash task.sh remove --lab "$lab" --version 2.0\nbash task.sh check --lab "$lab"\ncat "$lab_parent/sentinel.txt"\n```\n\nRequire that the inactive release is gone, the active version still runs,\narchives/evidence remain, and the outside sentinel is unchanged. The driver\nrejects unexpected release files, altered binary/manifest pairs, symlinks in\nowned directory paths and unrecognized active links. If a diagnostic occurs,\nkeep its exact output and status and resolve it before claiming success.\n\n## 5. Explain, retain and clean up\n\nComplete all five named note sections with predictions, raw commands/output,\nexit statuses, hashes, link targets, actual ownership results, changed version,\nrollback and cleanup observations. Read `operations.jsonl` for paired command\nlifecycles and their actual output. Compare a different run in a fresh lab\ninstead of replacing the first evidence.\n\nRemove release 1.0 only after saving the record. This removes the active link\nand that release, while leaving archives and the command log for inspection.\nA subsequent `check` fails because no release is active. Retain the\nnotes and evidence before deleting only this exact printed disposable lab and\nits own parent/sentinel. The collector does not claim worksheet completion.\nThe numbered observations support independent reading or a paced walkthrough.\n\nPrimary reference: [dpkg-query path ownership](https://manpages.debian.org/trixie/dpkg/dpkg-query.1.en.html).\n',
	"LS-20-unit-9-package-management-and-software-layout-supplemental-3":
		'# Dependency Failure, Installed State and Recovery\n\nThis optional Unit 9 extension follows the installed-package investigation.\nUse actual dpkg operations to distinguish an unpacked application, a satisfied\ndependency, a configured package and a working program. It has its own attempt\nand evidence record, separate from the manual-release comparison.\n\n## Scope, equipment and prerequisites\n\nUse an ordinary account in an owned disposable Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab with\nPython 3, Bash, `dpkg`, `dpkg-deb` and `dpkg-query`. The driver constructs three\ntiny local packages from the supplied source: base 1.0, base 2.0 and app 1.0.\nThe app declares `Depends: linux-layout-base (>= 2.0)`. No repository download,\nmaintainer script, trigger, root-owned payload or privileged operation is part\nof these packages. The host\'s installed package database is not the experiment.\n\nThe installation root and its database live below one newly created owned\nlab. Every dpkg change supplies that root explicitly and records its command\nand status. `--force-not-root` permits this ordinary-account demonstration;\nit does not waive dependency checks. Never drop the root option, copy these\noperations onto the host database, add sudo, or add `--force-depends`. Use the\nsupplied driver to keep the operation scoped. If the environment cannot run\nthis lab, retain the diagnostic and resolve its prerequisite rather than\npretending that an installation occurred.\n\nBoth roles provide working equipment. Complete the named observation tasks in\n`dependency-notes.md`; reference reasoning is not this machine\'s evidence.\nAfter confirming the IDE import, open those notes first, save/download the\nZIP, extract the role and use its Linux terminal. Browser saves do not execute\ndpkg operations. Return observations to the same saved attempt.\n\n## Package dependencies and state\n\nPackage metadata describes a version requirement. Here app 1.0 requires base\n2.0 or later. Installing an archive can unpack its files before configuration\nfails. File presence and even a successful direct program run therefore do not\nby themselves establish `install ok installed` state. Read the actual database\nstate separately. dpkg installs supplied archives and checks relationships;\nit does not fetch a missing dependency. APT can plan a repository transaction\nand resolve related changes, using its configured metadata and policy.\n\n## 1. Predict and construct the local packages\n\nPredict the first app install\'s status, whether its file will exist afterward,\nits database state, and what will be needed for recovery. Then run:\n\n```bash\nuname -s\nid -u\ndpkg --version\npython3 --version\nlab_parent=$(mktemp -d)\nlab="$lab_parent/dependencies"\nbash task.sh init --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh build --lab "$lab"\ndpkg-deb --field "$lab/packages/linux-layout-app-1.0.deb" Package Version Depends\ndpkg-deb --contents "$lab/packages/linux-layout-app-1.0.deb"\n```\n\nKeep the shell open and save the exact `OWNED_LAB` path. The package build\nretains this lab account\'s file ownership and writes a manifest of archive\nhashes. It refuses an existing workspace/build; each changed experiment uses\na new lab. Package structure and installed state are different observations.\n\n## 2. Observe the missing dependency without hiding failure\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh install-app --lab "$lab"\nprintf \'INSTALL_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"\nbash task.sh query --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh audit --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh run --lab "$lab"\nprintf \'RUNTIME_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"\n```\n\nExpect a nonzero install status and an app that is unpacked but not configured.\n`query` asks the separate database for both packages; its own status can be\nnonzero while the base is absent. It can also succeed when the dependency name\nhas a recorded `not-installed` entry. Query success means an entry was found,\nnot that its package is installed. Read the status field and confirm that the\nbase payload is absent. Retain stdout, stderr and status separately.\n`audit` identifies the incomplete package. The unpacked program reports\na missing dependency file and exit 1. This failure is an intended observation,\nnot a reason to continue as though installation succeeded.\n\n## 3. Distinguish an installed wrong version from a sufficient one\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh install-base --lab "$lab" --version 1.0\nbash task.sh configure-app --lab "$lab"\nprintf \'CONFIGURE_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"\nbash task.sh query --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh run --lab "$lab"\nprintf \'RUNTIME_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"\n```\n\nBase 1.0 can be installed successfully, while app configuration still fails\nits `>= 2.0` requirement. The app remains unpacked. Its direct runtime check\nseparately rejects base 1.0 with status 2. Record those distinct statuses and\nreasons. A dependency\'s name being present is not enough to satisfy a version\nconstraint. Do not edit the installed status database to make it look healthy.\n\n## 4. Upgrade, check runtime, then configure and audit\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh install-base --lab "$lab" --version 2.0\nbash task.sh query --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh run --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh configure-app --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh query --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh audit --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh run --lab "$lab"\n```\n\nObserve the app\'s actual database state before and after explicit\nconfiguration. With base 2.0 the supplied program prints\n`dependency-demo ready base=2.0` and exits 0, including before the app\'s\nconfiguration step. That pre-configuration run deliberately demonstrates\nthat runtime success and fully installed metadata are different claims.\nAfter configuration, require both packages to show `install ok installed`,\nan empty consistency audit and another successful behavioral run.\n\nThese packages have no service unit or persistent service process. No service\nrestart or reboot is established by this experiment. A real patch plan must\nidentify such effects for the real application, preserve configuration/data,\nretain recovery evidence and run its actual post-change acceptance check.\n\n## 5. Observe dependent removal and cleanly undo the lab\n\nPredict what happens if the installed base is removed while the app\nstill depends on it. Then run and immediately retain the status:\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh remove-base --lab "$lab"\nprintf \'REMOVE_BASE_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$?"\nbash task.sh query --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh run --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh remove-app --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh remove-base --lab "$lab"\nbash task.sh audit --lab "$lab"\n```\n\nRequire the first removal to fail without breaking the working installed\npair. The base can now show `deinstall ok installed`: its requested action\nchanged, while its current state and files remain installed. Read all three\nstatus fields; the requested action does not prove removal. Remove the\ndependent first, then the base. Verify the app and base\npayload paths are gone and the separate database has no installed lab\npackages. Record archive, dpkg-log and operation-log retention separately\nfrom removal of installed payloads. Keep an outside sentinel unchanged.\n\nComplete all five note sections with predictions and real query/audit/runtime\nrecords. Preserve `dpkg.log` and `operations.jsonl`; the latter contains paired\ncommand lifecycles, outputs and actual statuses. Repeat a changed sequence in\na fresh lab, such as installing base 2.0 before the app, and explain why its\nfirst app install succeeds. Failed commands never become a success flag for\nthe whole investigation. After saving evidence, delete only the exact printed\ndisposable workspace and its own parent. Use the same numbered sequence for\nindependent study or pauses in a guided walkthrough.\n\nPrimary references: [dpkg states and scoped installation options](https://manpages.debian.org/trixie/dpkg/dpkg.1.en.html)\nand [Debian dependency metadata](https://www.debian.org/doc/manuals/debian-handbook/sect.package-meta-information.en.html).\n',
	"LS-11-unit-10-networking-from-a-systems-view":
		'# Interface, Route, Listener and Application Evidence\n\nThis required Unit 10 project replaces networking guesses with observations at\nseparate layers. Complete `network-notes.md` while retaining each raw JSON\nrecord. The same prediction, observation and explanation pauses work for\nindependent reading and a paced walkthrough.\n\n## Preparation and roles\n\nUse an ordinary account in a Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab or WSL distribution.\nThe lab needs Python 3, Bash, iproute2 (`ip`, `ss`), iputils `ping`, and curl\n7.76 or later. The DNS continuation also needs BIND `dig`. Arrange for these\ntools to be available before the experiment. No Python packages are installed.\nThe website IDE edits, saves and exports these files; run the commands in the\nexternal Linux lab after extracting the ZIP. The equipment rejects a non-Linux\nhost, root, non-loopback destinations and invalid port numbers.\n\nKeep two terminals in the same selected role folder. `serve` occupies terminal A\nand prints a ready record containing its actual address, port and PID. The\nkernel selects an available port; never assume the example from another run.\nCtrl-C in that terminal closes the owned listener. `inspect` in terminal B\nrecords actual commands, statuses, output, timestamps and process identities.\nUse a new report name for each case. An existing report, including a symlink,\nis preserved. A completed private report has mode 0600 and appears only after\nall observations have been collected.\n\nThe collector returns 0 when the record was saved, including a recorded failed\nrequest. That status is not a claim that the network is healthy or the worksheet\nis complete. Read the request\'s `curlStatus`, `httpStatus`, body and error text.\nInput or platform rejection returns 2; an equipment/report failure returns 1.\nThe starter and reference contain identical working equipment. Normal work is\nthe five named tasks in the notes, not rewriting the supplied Python services.\nThe reference explains a method; it cannot supply the current lab\'s live PIDs,\nports, paths, packet results or your initial prediction.\n\n## Read the layers\n\nAn interface is a local network device; an address is assigned to an interface.\nThe `lo` loopback device carries traffic within this host. `ip -j address show\ndev lo` records its flags and address/prefix entries. A loopback operstate of\nUNKNOWN does not by itself mean that it is down. Read the flags and the actual\ntest too. The main route table and `ip -j route get 127.0.0.1` answer different\nquestions: configured main-table routes versus the kernel\'s selected local\npath. A selected route describes a proposed path, not successful delivery.\n\nA listening TCP socket has an address, port and owning process. `ss -H -ltnp`\nlists listeners without name resolution and includes visible process ownership.\nThe supplied collector filters by the exact observed port. A healthy interface\nand a successful ICMP echo do not prove that a particular TCP service is alive.\nHTTP adds another layer: connecting to a server can still return a missing\npath or an application failure.\n\n## Predict, run and compare\n\n1. Before starting a server, predict which layer names the address, route,\n   listening process and application result. Record that prediction as task 1.\n2. In terminal A run `bash task.sh serve`. Keep it running. Note the actual\n   address, port and PID from its ready line.\n3. In terminal B enter `read -r -p \'Port from the ready line: \' port`, then type\n   that number. Run `bash task.sh inspect --port "$port" --report first.json`.\n   Open `first.json`. Find the `lo` address, selected route, exact listener,\n   ICMP observation, and HTTP status/body. `/health` returns 200 and a body\n   containing `ready` and the server PID. Compare that PID with the ready line\n   and `ss`; do not confuse the collector PID with the serving process.\n4. Independently run `ip -j address show dev lo`, `ip -j route get 127.0.0.1`,\n   `ss -H -ltnp "sport = :$port"`, and\n   `curl --noproxy \'*\' --fail-with-body "http://127.0.0.1:$port/health"`.\n   Compare these actual outputs with the corresponding saved observations.\n5. Predict a request to an absent path, then run\n   `bash task.sh inspect --port "$port" --path /missing --report missing.json`.\n   Expect HTTP 404, curl status 22, and `missing` with the same server PID.\n   This is an HTTP response from a listening server, not evidence of a dead port.\n6. Stop terminal A with Ctrl-C. Confirm the exact listener is gone using `ss`.\n   Run `bash task.sh inspect --port "$port" --report stopped.json`. In this\n   controlled lab, expect curl status 7, HTTP status 000 (stored as integer 0),\n   and no HTTP body. The interface/route may remain healthy. If another process\n   has since acquired that port, record that actual observation instead of\n   forcing the expected answer. Start a fresh owned server for another trial.\n7. Start a fresh server, enter its new actual port and collect `restored.json`.\n   Demonstrate HTTP 200 and the new listener PID. Preserve all earlier records.\n\nThe equipment disables curl proxies and uses literal 127.0.0.1/127.0.0.2 targets.\nNo public listener, route edit, firewall edit or internet request is needed.\nAn ICMP permission error in a restricted environment is an observation about\nthe available test, not proof that the HTTP service is down.\n\n## Complete and retain\n\nFinish all five notes tasks with an initial prediction, actual record/field\ncitations, your independent comparison, the stopped-service changed case, and\nrecovery/cleanup evidence. State the narrowest conclusion each tool supports.\nDownload/reopen the saved workspace and keep the reports with the final notes.\nRunning the reference does not complete the learner\'s investigation.\n\n## Optional transfer\n\nThe listener continuation isolates wrong bind addresses, absent paths and\napplication readiness. The DNS continuation deliberately separates a DNS answer\nfrom an explicit HTTP address override. These are distinct optional experiments\nafter this core checkpoint, not three copies of one log-summary script.\n\nPrimary references: [ip](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html),\n[ss](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html),\n[ping](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ping.8.html), and\n[curl options and exit codes](https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html).\n',
	"LS-21-unit-10-networking-from-a-systems-view-supplemental-2":
		'# Listener Failure versus an HTTP Error\n\nThis optional transfer follows the required interface/route investigation.\nKeep the same local path while changing one service property at a time.\nComplete `listener-notes.md`; do not replace evidence with a generic list of\nnetworking commands or change the host\'s networking configuration.\n\n## Equipment and recording\n\nUse an ordinary account in a Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab or WSL distribution.\nThe lab needs Python 3, Bash, iproute2 (`ip`, `ss`), iputils `ping`, and curl\n7.76 or later. The DNS continuation also needs BIND `dig`. Arrange for these\ntools to be available before the experiment. No Python packages are installed.\nThe website IDE edits, saves and exports these files; run the commands in the\nexternal Linux lab after extracting the ZIP. The equipment rejects a non-Linux\nhost, root, non-loopback destinations and invalid port numbers.\n\nKeep two terminals in the same selected role folder. `serve` occupies terminal A\nand prints a ready record containing its actual address, port and PID. The\nkernel selects an available port; never assume the example from another run.\nCtrl-C in that terminal closes the owned listener. `inspect` in terminal B\nrecords actual commands, statuses, output, timestamps and process identities.\nUse a new report name for each case. An existing report, including a symlink,\nis preserved. A completed private report has mode 0600 and appears only after\nall observations have been collected.\n\nThe collector returns 0 when the record was saved, including a recorded failed\nrequest. That status is not a claim that the network is healthy or the worksheet\nis complete. Read the request\'s `curlStatus`, `httpStatus`, body and error text.\nInput or platform rejection returns 2; an equipment/report failure returns 1.\nThe starter and reference contain identical working equipment. Normal work is\nthe five named tasks in the notes, not rewriting the supplied Python services.\nThe reference explains a method; it cannot supply the current lab\'s live PIDs,\nports, paths, packet results or your initial prediction.\n\n## Distinguish the cases before running them\n\nThe bind address belongs to the listening socket. A connection aimed at\n127.0.0.1 does not reach a socket bound only to 127.0.0.2, even when the numeric\nport is equal. Linux normally routes both through loopback. Compare the exact\nlocal address in `ss`, the selected destination, and the owning PID before\nguessing a missing route or firewall rule. `ping` tests ICMP, not that TCP port.\n\nThe supplied HTTP service has two modes. Ready mode returns 200 at `/health`;\nunavailable mode returns 503 there. Both return 404 for `/missing`. Every body\nincludes the actual serving PID. With `--fail-with-body`, curl retains 4xx/5xx\nbodies but returns 22. A refused TCP connection instead has curl status 7 and\nno HTTP response. The collector saves these distinct outcomes without turning\nthem into a fabricated all-pass summary.\n\n## Controlled sequence\n\n1. In notes task 1, predict the first failing layer and expected `ss`, curl\n   status and HTTP status for each case below. Keep your initial prediction.\n2. Terminal A: `bash task.sh serve --bind 127.0.0.2`. In terminal B enter\n   `read -r -p \'Port from the ready line: \' port`. Then run\n   `bash task.sh inspect --port "$port" --address 127.0.0.1 --report wrong-bind.json`.\n   The listener exists at .2, while this request targets .1. Identify that\n   mismatch using actual addresses rather than calling every failed request DNS.\n3. With that same server still running, use\n   `bash task.sh inspect --port "$port" --address 127.0.0.2 --report changed-target.json`.\n   Expect HTTP 200. Explain why this diagnostic client change proves a reachable\n   endpoint but does not move the server\'s bind address.\n4. Stop the .2 server. Start `bash task.sh serve --bind 127.0.0.1` and enter its\n   actual new port. Save `ready.json`, then run\n   `bash task.sh inspect --port "$port" --path /missing --report missing-path.json`.\n   Compare HTTP 200 at `/health` with 404 at `/missing`, the retained body and\n   the same listening process. A missing URL is not a missing TCP listener.\n5. Stop the server. Start `bash task.sh serve --mode unavailable`, enter its\n   port and save `unavailable.json`. The expected result is HTTP 503 with curl\n   status 22 and an `unavailable` body. The process and socket still exist.\n   State what this controlled fixture proves and what a real 503 would still\n   require examining, such as application dependencies and its own logs.\n6. Stop that server, start ready mode again and save `recovered.json` using the\n   new port. Independently verify the exact listener/PID with\n   `ss -H -ltnp "sport = :$port"` and request `/health` using curl. Finally stop\n   it and verify the owned listener disappears. Keep all case records.\n\n## Explain the repair\n\nComplete the five notes tasks: predictions, bind mismatch evidence and changed\ntarget, path comparison, application readiness, and recovery/cleanup. State\nwhich correction belongs to the client destination, listening configuration,\nURL path or application behavior. The reference describes these relationships\nwithout inventing a server PID or treating every HTTP error as a network outage.\nNormal completion requires the actual changed cases and a corrected local\nrequest. No proxy, system service, firewall or public exposure is introduced.\n\nPrimary references: [ss socket filters](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html)\nand [curl error/body behavior](https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html).\n',
	"LS-22-unit-10-networking-from-a-systems-view-supplemental-3":
		"# Controlled DNS Diagnosis and Explicit Address Bypass\n\nThis optional extension separates a DNS answer, an address selection and an\nHTTP request. Complete `resolver-notes.md` after the core networking checkpoint.\nThe supplied DNS implementation is complete equipment, not a request to write\na recursive resolver or replace the host's resolver configuration.\n\n## Preparation and processes\n\nUse an ordinary account in a Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab or WSL distribution.\nThe lab needs Python 3, Bash, iproute2 (`ip`, `ss`), iputils `ping`, and curl\n7.76 or later. The DNS continuation also needs BIND `dig`. Arrange for these\ntools to be available before the experiment. No Python packages are installed.\nThe website IDE edits, saves and exports these files; run the commands in the\nexternal Linux lab after extracting the ZIP. The equipment rejects a non-Linux\nhost, root, non-loopback destinations and invalid port numbers.\n\nKeep two terminals in the same selected role folder. `serve` occupies terminal A\nand prints a ready record containing its actual address, port and PID. The\nkernel selects an available port; never assume the example from another run.\nCtrl-C in that terminal closes the owned listener. `inspect` in terminal B\nrecords actual commands, statuses, output, timestamps and process identities.\nUse a new report name for each case. An existing report, including a symlink,\nis preserved. A completed private report has mode 0600 and appears only after\nall observations have been collected.\n\nThe collector returns 0 when the record was saved, including a recorded failed\nrequest. That status is not a claim that the network is healthy or the worksheet\nis complete. Read the request's `curlStatus`, `httpStatus`, body and error text.\nInput or platform rejection returns 2; an equipment/report failure returns 1.\nThe starter and reference contain identical working equipment. Normal work is\nthe five named tasks in the notes, not rewriting the supplied Python services.\nThe reference explains a method; it cannot supply the current lab's live PIDs,\nports, paths, packet results or your initial prediction.\n\nUse three terminals in the selected role folder. Terminal A runs HTTP:\n`bash task.sh serve`. Terminal B runs the controlled DNS service:\n`bash task.sh dns --answer ready`. Both print independently selected ports and\nPIDs. In terminal C enter `read -r -p 'HTTP port: ' http_port` and\n`read -r -p 'DNS port: ' dns_port` using those actual ready lines. The DNS\nfixture listens only on 127.0.0.1 UDP at its unprivileged port, not public port\n53. It answers a single ordinary IN/A question for `lab.invalid.` with TTL 0.\nThe supported diagnostic uses UDP with EDNS and recursion disabled. This small\nfixture does not implement general recursion, caching, TCP fallback or DNSSEC.\n\n## Predict and separate observations\n\n1. Predict how a valid address, an incorrect address and NXDOMAIN differ\n   in the DNS record and the later HTTP request. Record the prediction first.\n2. With ready DNS running, use\n   `bash task.sh inspect --dns-port \"$dns_port\" --http-port \"$http_port\" --report ready.json`.\n   Find DNS status NOERROR, one A answer 127.0.0.1, the actual UDP listener PID,\n   the HTTP listener PID, and HTTP 200. Independently inspect the answer using\n   `dig -r @127.0.0.1 -p \"$dns_port\" lab.invalid. A +noedns +norecurse +notcp +nosearch +time=1 +tries=1`.\n   An explicit server/port and final dot make this a query of the owned fixture;\n   it does not test the host's ordinary name-service search path.\n3. Leave HTTP running. Stop only terminal B and restart DNS with\n   `bash task.sh dns --answer wrong`. Enter its new DNS port in terminal C and\n   collect `wrong-answer.json`. NOERROR now supplies 127.0.0.2. The request\n   following that selected answer fails to connect to the .1 HTTP\n   listener, while `explicitBypassRequest` still returns 200 at .1. Cite both\n   results. DNS query success alone does not prove that its answer is correct.\n4. Stop only DNS, restart `bash task.sh dns --answer missing`, enter its actual\n   port and collect `nxdomain.json`. The DNS response says NXDOMAIN. `dig` can\n   exit 0 for this valid negative response. The collector does not attempt the\n   selected-address HTTP request because no A answer exists; that field is null.\n   It separately performs the explicit .1 bypass to test the unchanged app.\n5. Restore DNS using `--answer ready`, enter the new DNS port and collect\n   `restored.json`. Independently query that server again and verify the ready\n   answer plus a successful selected-address HTTP request. Stop both foreground\n   servers, and use `ss -H -lunp \"sport = :$dns_port\"` and\n   `ss -H -ltnp \"sport = :$http_port\"` to confirm their owned sockets are gone.\n\n## What the bypass does\n\nFor the HTTP experiment the collector supplies the observed fixture address\nto curl's `--resolve lab.invalid:PORT:ADDRESS`, with proxies disabled. Curl\ntherefore uses the explicit address for that one host/port pair. This makes\nthe relationship between the captured DNS answer and connection test visible;\nit does not route curl's DNS lookups through the fixture automatically.\nThe separate .1 override can demonstrate a healthy app despite bad/missing\nDNS, but is not evidence that the system resolver was repaired. This exercise\ndoes not edit `/etc/hosts`, `/etc/resolv.conf`, routes, firewall rules or global\nDNS. The repair being demonstrated is the owned DNS fixture's restored answer.\n\n## Complete and retain\n\nFinish the five notes tasks with the original prediction, independent DNS\ncomparison, incorrect-answer case, negative-answer case and restored/cleaned\nstate. Identify both process PIDs and both ports from actual records. Keep\nclaims about global resolution, TCP service reachability and HTTP status\nseparate. Reopen the saved workspace and retain all four reports with the notes.\n\nPrimary references: [BIND dig](https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/latest/manpages.html#dig-dns-lookup-utility),\n[curl --resolve](https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--resolve), and\n[RFC 1035](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1035.html) for the restricted\nDNS packet equipment.\n",
	"LS-12-unit-12-storage-backups-and-reliability":
		'# Capacity Evidence, Verified Backup and Separate Restore\n\nThis is the required Unit 12 project. Investigate actual capacity and a separate verified restoration before optional continuations. The notebook continues the same core attempt.\n\n## Environment and saved work\n\nUse an ordinary account in a Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab or WSL distribution with\nPython 3, Bash, GNU tar and GNU coreutils (`df`, `du`, `sha256sum`). Arrange for\nmissing tools before starting. No package installation is part of this lab.\nThe site IDE edits, saves and exports these sources and notes. Confirm the\nselected role\'s import, download its ZIP, extract it and run commands from the\nrole folder in the external Linux terminal. Browser saving does not run tar.\n\nThe starter and reference have identical working equipment. Complete the five\nnamed tasks in the notes using predictions and actual evidence; the reference\nexplains a method without fabricating this lab\'s paths, sizes, timings or hashes.\nUse the same numbered pauses for independent reading and a paced walkthrough.\nAn equipment exit of 0 does not complete the notes. Errors are separate: invalid\narguments, root, the wrong platform, an existing name or a stale policy return\n2; missing tools, failed native commands or corrupt backup evidence return 1.\nAn ordinary handled interruption returns 130.\n\nCreate a fresh private parent and keep this shell open:\n\n```bash\nuname -s\nid -u\npython3 --version\ntar --version\ndf --version\nlab_parent=$(mktemp -d)\nlab="$lab_parent/storage"\nbash task.sh init --lab "$lab"\n```\n\nRecord the exact printed absolute `lab` path. Initialization refuses an existing\npath. All commands require this account\'s matching lab marker and regular files\nor directories. Keep this directory private; do not substitute real home,\nshared, system or production data. The small supplied dataset has a text\nversion, a binary file, a filename containing a space, an empty directory, a\n1 MiB sparse file and an explicitly excluded transient `cache` directory.\n\nThe archive contains `data` contents except `cache`. Its manifest records file\nnames, types, content hashes, sizes, account ownership and permission modes,\nplus archive SHA-256, sequence, source path and the capacity observation.\n`backup` first checks currently available bytes against apparent input size,\narchive overhead and a configurable reserve (16 MiB by default). Space can\nchange after that check. The actual tar status and complete inventory check\nremain necessary. A partial staging directory is discarded after a handled\ncommand failure or interruption; earlier published backups remain in place.\nA power loss or SIGKILL can leave staging evidence, which requires inspection.\nIt never counts as a valid backup.\n\nArchive checks reject unsupported paths, links, special files, extra/missing\nmembers, content mismatches and special permission bits. Restore uses actual\nGNU tar only after validation, into a new directory under `restores`. It\nrefuses an existing destination and compares restored bytes, paths, ownership\nand modes with the saved inventory. The original source is checked separately.\nFailures do not become empty successful summaries.\n\nCommands, PIDs, start/end times, statuses, stdout, stderr and child cleanup are\nretained in private `operations.jsonl`. Keep that record with the notes, source\nobservations, manifests, trusted checksum record and restored comparison.\n\n## Scope of the evidence\n\n`df` observes the containing mounted filesystem. `du` observes the selected tree;\nits apparent-size mode counts logical lengths rather than allocated blocks.\nThe sparse file can therefore have a large logical length and few allocated\nbytes. Read actual results instead of assuming the two totals match. Inode\navailability is another resource, separate from available bytes. The plain tar\narchive need not preserve the original sparse allocation, even when restored\ncontent and length match.\n\nThese owned archives use the same filesystem as the source. They demonstrate\nfile recovery, not survival of that disk\'s failure. A real backup plan needs\nan independently protected copy, explicit recovery point/time requirements,\naccess control and repeated restoration tests. RAID, replication and a copied\nfile alone do not establish that recovery requirement. The manifest here is\nan integrity reference, not a signature: changing both archive and manifest\ncan defeat a self-contained checksum claim. Retain a trusted checksum record\nseparately. This regular-file lab does not establish database consistency or\npreservation of ACLs, xattrs, links, device files or a bootable system.\n\n## 1. Predict and observe capacity\n\nPredict which command describes a filesystem, a directory tree, logical file\nlength and inode availability. Run `bash task.sh observe --lab "$lab" --report\nfirst` and open `reports/first.json`. The four recorded commands are actual df\nand du queries. Independently repeat the displayed commands and compare their\nscope, units and current output. Read `sparseFile.size` and `allocatedBytes`.\nRecord actual mount identity, available bytes and inodes; do not invent totals.\nReusing `--report first` refuses replacement and preserves its original bytes.\n\n## 2. Create and independently inspect a backup\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name initial\nbash task.sh list --lab "$lab" --name initial\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name initial\nsha256sum -- "$lab/backups/initial/archive.tar"\n```\n\nCompare the independent checksum with `manifest.json`. Find every declared\nfile, the empty directory and its mode; confirm `cache` is absent from the\narchive while its source file still exists. A listing proves readable archive\nstructure, while verification also checks content and the declared member set.\nKeep this initial backup and manifest. An existing backup name is refused.\n\n## 3. Change live data, then restore the earlier state separately\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh change --lab "$lab" --text \'version two\'\nbash task.sh restore --lab "$lab" --name initial --destination recovered-first\ncat "$lab/data/report.txt"\ncat "$lab/restores/recovered-first/report.txt"\n```\n\nPredict those two contents before reading them. The live source remains\n`version two`; the separate restore contains `version one`. Compare the full\nmanifest, byte hashes, file modes and account ownership, including binary\ncontent, the space in the filename and the empty directory. Restoring an older\nversion does not make the current source older or the backup current.\n\n## 4. Observe a capacity rejection without filling the disk\n\nThe following requests a reserve larger than currently available space:\n\n```bash\nreserve=$(python3 -c \'import os,sys; v=os.statvfs(sys.argv[1]); print(v.f_bavail*v.f_frsize+1024*1024*1024)\' "$lab")\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name no-space --reserve-bytes "$reserve"\ncapacity_status=$?\nprintf \'CAPACITY_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$capacity_status"\n```\n\nRead the actual available/needed byte message and status 1. Confirm no\n`backups/no-space` or partial staging candidate exists and `initial` retains\nits original bytes. This is an explicit reserve rejection based on a real\ncapacity observation, not a test that fills a filesystem to exhaustion.\n\n## 5. Make and restore a changed backup\n\nCreate `--name changed`, restore it to `--destination recovered-second` and\nconfirm the second restore contains `version two`. Check the first restore\nand original backup still retain their earlier state. Retry an existing\nrestore destination and retain its refusal plus unchanged restored bytes.\nRecord the source/exclusion list, two recovery points, trusted checksums and\nactual restore evidence. The troubleshooting notebook continues this same\nattempt rather than creating another program.\n\nAfter saving evidence, remove only the exact disposable lab and private\nparent created above. Never substitute a home directory or storage device.\nThe optional corruption and retention labs use separate fresh attempts.\n\nPrimary references: [GNU df](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/df.1.html),\n[GNU du](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/du.1.html),\n[GNU tar](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html) and\n[GNU sha256sum](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sha256sum.1.html).\n',
	"LS-25-unit-12-storage-backups-and-reliability-supplemental-2":
		'# Archive Listing versus Content Integrity\n\nThis optional transfer follows a successful separate restore. Isolate archive readability, content integrity, refusal and recovery in its own attempt.\n\n## Environment and saved work\n\nUse an ordinary account in a Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab or WSL distribution with\nPython 3, Bash, GNU tar and GNU coreutils (`df`, `du`, `sha256sum`). Arrange for\nmissing tools before starting. No package installation is part of this lab.\nThe site IDE edits, saves and exports these sources and notes. Confirm the\nselected role\'s import, download its ZIP, extract it and run commands from the\nrole folder in the external Linux terminal. Browser saving does not run tar.\n\nThe starter and reference have identical working equipment. Complete the five\nnamed tasks in the notes using predictions and actual evidence; the reference\nexplains a method without fabricating this lab\'s paths, sizes, timings or hashes.\nUse the same numbered pauses for independent reading and a paced walkthrough.\nAn equipment exit of 0 does not complete the notes. Errors are separate: invalid\narguments, root, the wrong platform, an existing name or a stale policy return\n2; missing tools, failed native commands or corrupt backup evidence return 1.\nAn ordinary handled interruption returns 130.\n\nCreate a fresh private parent and keep this shell open:\n\n```bash\nuname -s\nid -u\npython3 --version\ntar --version\ndf --version\nlab_parent=$(mktemp -d)\nlab="$lab_parent/storage"\nbash task.sh init --lab "$lab"\n```\n\nRecord the exact printed absolute `lab` path. Initialization refuses an existing\npath. All commands require this account\'s matching lab marker and regular files\nor directories. Keep this directory private; do not substitute real home,\nshared, system or production data. The small supplied dataset has a text\nversion, a binary file, a filename containing a space, an empty directory, a\n1 MiB sparse file and an explicitly excluded transient `cache` directory.\n\nThe archive contains `data` contents except `cache`. Its manifest records file\nnames, types, content hashes, sizes, account ownership and permission modes,\nplus archive SHA-256, sequence, source path and the capacity observation.\n`backup` first checks currently available bytes against apparent input size,\narchive overhead and a configurable reserve (16 MiB by default). Space can\nchange after that check. The actual tar status and complete inventory check\nremain necessary. A partial staging directory is discarded after a handled\ncommand failure or interruption; earlier published backups remain in place.\nA power loss or SIGKILL can leave staging evidence, which requires inspection.\nIt never counts as a valid backup.\n\nArchive checks reject unsupported paths, links, special files, extra/missing\nmembers, content mismatches and special permission bits. Restore uses actual\nGNU tar only after validation, into a new directory under `restores`. It\nrefuses an existing destination and compares restored bytes, paths, ownership\nand modes with the saved inventory. The original source is checked separately.\nFailures do not become empty successful summaries.\n\nCommands, PIDs, start/end times, statuses, stdout, stderr and child cleanup are\nretained in private `operations.jsonl`. Keep that record with the notes, source\nobservations, manifests, trusted checksum record and restored comparison.\n\n## Scope of the evidence\n\n`df` observes the containing mounted filesystem. `du` observes the selected tree;\nits apparent-size mode counts logical lengths rather than allocated blocks.\nThe sparse file can therefore have a large logical length and few allocated\nbytes. Read actual results instead of assuming the two totals match. Inode\navailability is another resource, separate from available bytes. The plain tar\narchive need not preserve the original sparse allocation, even when restored\ncontent and length match.\n\nThese owned archives use the same filesystem as the source. They demonstrate\nfile recovery, not survival of that disk\'s failure. A real backup plan needs\nan independently protected copy, explicit recovery point/time requirements,\naccess control and repeated restoration tests. RAID, replication and a copied\nfile alone do not establish that recovery requirement. The manifest here is\nan integrity reference, not a signature: changing both archive and manifest\ncan defeat a self-contained checksum claim. Retain a trusted checksum record\nseparately. This regular-file lab does not establish database consistency or\npreservation of ACLs, xattrs, links, device files or a bootable system.\n\n## 1. Establish a trustworthy comparison\n\nAfter the fresh initialization, predict what a successful archive listing\ndoes and does not prove. Run:\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name baseline\nbash task.sh list --lab "$lab" --name baseline\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name baseline\nbash task.sh restore --lab "$lab" --name baseline --destination trusted-copy\nsha256sum -- "$lab/backups/baseline/archive.tar"\n```\n\nSave the actual manifest/checksum outside\nthe archive and compare every restored entry. Keep this good backup untouched.\n\n## 2. Change content without breaking the tar listing\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name damaged\nbash task.sh damage --lab "$lab" --name damaged --mode flip\nbash task.sh list --lab "$lab" --name damaged\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name damaged\nverify_status=$?\nprintf \'VERIFY_STATUS=%s\\n\' "$verify_status"\n```\n\nThe deliberate flip changes a byte in the archived text payload; its header\nand names remain readable. Expect a successful listing but a nonzero integrity\ncheck (1). Compare the changed archive SHA-256 with its retained manifest.\nDo not rewrite the manifest to manufacture a pass. This controlled fault\nexplains why archive readability alone does not prove correct backup content.\n\n## 3. Observe the restoration gate\n\nRun `bash task.sh restore --lab "$lab" --name damaged --destination refused`.\nCapture status 1 and the actual mismatch error. Confirm `restores/refused`\nwas never created, live data is unchanged and the trusted baseline and restore\nremain byte-identical. Source availability and a valid earlier copy are separate\nobservations from this failed candidate.\n\n## 4. Compare a truncated archive\n\nCreate and damage another fresh archive:\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name truncated\nbash task.sh damage --lab "$lab" --name truncated --mode truncate\nbash task.sh list --lab "$lab" --name truncated\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name truncated\nbash task.sh restore --lab "$lab" --name truncated --destination truncated-refused\n```\n\nCapture each status immediately with `$?`. Compare its listing failure,\nverification refusal and rejected restore to a new destination. Retain actual\nstatuses rather than treating a tar diagnostic as a completed investigation.\nThe truncated archive and the readable-but-changed archive are different cases.\n\n## 5. Recover from a fresh verified artifact\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh change --lab "$lab" --text \'repaired version\'\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name repaired\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name repaired\nbash task.sh restore --lab "$lab" --name repaired --destination recovered\n```\n\nCompare real restored bytes,\nmode and current account with the new manifest and source. The repaired backup\nis a new artifact; it does not repair the damaged historical bytes. Preserve\nboth failed records and the original good baseline. Complete all five notes\nwith initial predictions, independent checksum/listing results, refusal before\ndestination creation, changed-case evidence and recovery/cleanup observations.\nSave evidence before removing only this exact disposable lab and parent.\n\nPrimary references: [GNU df](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/df.1.html),\n[GNU du](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/du.1.html),\n[GNU tar](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html) and\n[GNU sha256sum](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sha256sum.1.html).\n',
	"LS-26-unit-12-storage-backups-and-reliability-supplemental-3":
		'# Reviewed Retention and a Verified Recovery Window\n\nThis optional extension follows the core restore. Investigate an explicitly reviewed retention policy and the recovery points it preserves or removes in its own attempt.\n\n## Environment and saved work\n\nUse an ordinary account in a Debian/Ubuntu Linux lab or WSL distribution with\nPython 3, Bash, GNU tar and GNU coreutils (`df`, `du`, `sha256sum`). Arrange for\nmissing tools before starting. No package installation is part of this lab.\nThe site IDE edits, saves and exports these sources and notes. Confirm the\nselected role\'s import, download its ZIP, extract it and run commands from the\nrole folder in the external Linux terminal. Browser saving does not run tar.\n\nThe starter and reference have identical working equipment. Complete the five\nnamed tasks in the notes using predictions and actual evidence; the reference\nexplains a method without fabricating this lab\'s paths, sizes, timings or hashes.\nUse the same numbered pauses for independent reading and a paced walkthrough.\nAn equipment exit of 0 does not complete the notes. Errors are separate: invalid\narguments, root, the wrong platform, an existing name or a stale policy return\n2; missing tools, failed native commands or corrupt backup evidence return 1.\nAn ordinary handled interruption returns 130.\n\nCreate a fresh private parent and keep this shell open:\n\n```bash\nuname -s\nid -u\npython3 --version\ntar --version\ndf --version\nlab_parent=$(mktemp -d)\nlab="$lab_parent/storage"\nbash task.sh init --lab "$lab"\n```\n\nRecord the exact printed absolute `lab` path. Initialization refuses an existing\npath. All commands require this account\'s matching lab marker and regular files\nor directories. Keep this directory private; do not substitute real home,\nshared, system or production data. The small supplied dataset has a text\nversion, a binary file, a filename containing a space, an empty directory, a\n1 MiB sparse file and an explicitly excluded transient `cache` directory.\n\nThe archive contains `data` contents except `cache`. Its manifest records file\nnames, types, content hashes, sizes, account ownership and permission modes,\nplus archive SHA-256, sequence, source path and the capacity observation.\n`backup` first checks currently available bytes against apparent input size,\narchive overhead and a configurable reserve (16 MiB by default). Space can\nchange after that check. The actual tar status and complete inventory check\nremain necessary. A partial staging directory is discarded after a handled\ncommand failure or interruption; earlier published backups remain in place.\nA power loss or SIGKILL can leave staging evidence, which requires inspection.\nIt never counts as a valid backup.\n\nArchive checks reject unsupported paths, links, special files, extra/missing\nmembers, content mismatches and special permission bits. Restore uses actual\nGNU tar only after validation, into a new directory under `restores`. It\nrefuses an existing destination and compares restored bytes, paths, ownership\nand modes with the saved inventory. The original source is checked separately.\nFailures do not become empty successful summaries.\n\nCommands, PIDs, start/end times, statuses, stdout, stderr and child cleanup are\nretained in private `operations.jsonl`. Keep that record with the notes, source\nobservations, manifests, trusted checksum record and restored comparison.\n\n## Scope of the evidence\n\n`df` observes the containing mounted filesystem. `du` observes the selected tree;\nits apparent-size mode counts logical lengths rather than allocated blocks.\nThe sparse file can therefore have a large logical length and few allocated\nbytes. Read actual results instead of assuming the two totals match. Inode\navailability is another resource, separate from available bytes. The plain tar\narchive need not preserve the original sparse allocation, even when restored\ncontent and length match.\n\nThese owned archives use the same filesystem as the source. They demonstrate\nfile recovery, not survival of that disk\'s failure. A real backup plan needs\nan independently protected copy, explicit recovery point/time requirements,\naccess control and repeated restoration tests. RAID, replication and a copied\nfile alone do not establish that recovery requirement. The manifest here is\nan integrity reference, not a signature: changing both archive and manifest\ncan defeat a self-contained checksum claim. Retain a trusted checksum record\nseparately. This regular-file lab does not establish database consistency or\npreservation of ACLs, xattrs, links, device files or a bootable system.\n\n## 1. Define the recovery window before deleting anything\n\nPredict which versions a keep-two policy retains after three successful\nbackups. In this fresh lab, run:\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name v1\nbash task.sh change --lab "$lab" --text \'version two\'\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name v2\nbash task.sh change --lab "$lab" --text \'version three\'\nbash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name v3\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name v1\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name v2\nbash task.sh verify --lab "$lab" --name v3\nsha256sum -- "$lab"/backups/*/archive.tar\n```\n\nCompare all three independent checksums with the manifests.\nRead each manifest\'s sequence and source version. Ordering uses the explicit\ncreation sequence, not alphabetical filenames or a guess from display time.\n\n## 2. Review a plan that deletes nothing\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh plan --lab "$lab" --keep 2 --report first-plan\ncat "$lab/reports/first-plan.json"\n```\n\nThe plan records every archive and manifest hash, sequence, exact proposed\nremoval and retained set. Confirm all three backup directories still exist.\nExplain why `v1` is proposed for removal and what historical recovery point\nwould be lost. An existing report name is refused and keep-zero is rejected.\nA plan is a recorded proposal, not proof that any deletion happened.\n\n## 3. Make the plan stale and require a fresh decision\n\nRun `bash task.sh change --lab "$lab" --text \'version four\'` and\n`bash task.sh backup --lab "$lab" --name a-fourth`.\nIts filename sorts before `v1`, but its sequence is newer. Try\n`bash task.sh apply --lab "$lab" --report first-plan`; capture status 2 and the\nstale-plan refusal. Confirm all four archives remain unchanged. An added,\nchanged, corrupt or removed backup invalidates the captured state. A corrupt\narchive fails integrity verification (1) before selection or deletion.\n\n## 4. Apply only the freshly reviewed selection\n\nRun `bash task.sh plan --lab "$lab" --keep 2 --report current-plan`.\nRead and explain its proposed\nremoval of `v1` and `v2` and retention of `v3` and `a-fourth`. Then run:\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh apply --lab "$lab" --report current-plan\n```\n\nConfirm exactly the two selected old directories are gone and both retained\narchive/manifest pairs are byte-identical to their planned hashes. Source data,\nreports, earlier restored evidence and an outside sentinel remain unchanged.\nReapplying the old plan now fails because the current set differs; record the\nrefusal without hiding it. This lab intentionally removes only disposable\nbackup copies, and never selects the last verified archive for removal.\n\n## 5. Prove what the retained window can restore\n\n```bash\nbash task.sh restore --lab "$lab" --name v3 --destination recovered-third\nbash task.sh restore --lab "$lab" --name a-fourth --destination recovered-fourth\n```\n\nCompare\ntheir real texts `version three` and `version four`, binary file, empty\ndirectory, file modes and current ownership with each saved manifest. State\nexplicitly that a request for `version one` can no longer be satisfied from\nthese retained backups. A retention count cannot replace a restore test or an\nindependently protected copy. Complete the five notes with prediction, initial\nproposal, stale-plan rejection, exact deletion evidence and the two restored\nrecovery points. Save evidence before removing only this disposable lab and\nits exact private parent.\n\nPrimary references: [GNU df](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/df.1.html),\n[GNU du](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/du.1.html),\n[GNU tar](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html) and\n[GNU sha256sum](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sha256sum.1.html).\n',
	"LS-03-unit-1-shell-foundations-supplemental-2":
		"# Literal Search, Filename Boundaries and Three Outcomes\n\nThis optional transfer follows the required structured log-summary project.\nIt returns the names of text files containing a literal string. It does not\ncount severity fields, recursively discover files or edit input data. The\nnew work is a filename pipeline and the distinction between matches, no\nmatches and a failed search. Complete `search-notes.md` with actual evidence.\nThe prediction, inspection and explanation pauses support independent study\nand a paced walkthrough.\n\n## Equipment and stream contract\n\nUse an ordinary account in an owned Linux lab with Bash, GNU grep and GNU sort.\nExtract one role with its `records` folder. The site IDE edits, saves and exports\nthe workspace; run these commands in the external Linux lab after extraction.\nNo installation, system configuration or privileged access is part of the task.\n\n`task.sh` accepts a nonempty literal string and one or more explicit ordinary\nnonsymlink text-file paths. Each shell argument remains one filename. Quote\nspaces, wildcard characters and newlines. The script rejects a standalone `-`\noperand, directories, symbolic links and special files. A nonexistent or\nunreadable file reaches grep and produces an actual tool error.\n\nThe pipeline is `grep -F -l -Z -e TEXT -- FILE... | sort -z -u`. `-F` interprets\ntext literally; `-e` protects a pattern beginning with a dash, and `--` ends\noption parsing for the file operands. `-l` selects filenames rather than lines.\n`-Z` terminates each filename with NUL; `sort -z -u` preserves that separator,\nsorts the byte strings under the script's C locale, and removes repeated\nidentical path arguments. NUL cannot occur inside a Unix filename, while a\nnewline can. This does not merge different paths that happen to name the same\nunderlying file. Do not store a NUL stream in shell command substitution.\n\nThe reference returns 0 when at least one file matches and both tools succeed,\n1 when no file matches and sorting succeeds, and 2 for invalid arguments or a\ntool failure. stderr separately reports `grep=N sort=N`. stdout can contain\nsome valid names before an input fails. Status 2 makes that list incomplete;\ndiscard it as a complete result. `grep -q` is deliberately absent because its\nearly success can conceal an error after a match.\n\n## Predict, run and inspect the filename stream\n\nFrom the selected role folder, predict the literal matches for `ERROR[42]`.\nThe supplied `alpha.txt` and `-dash.txt` contain that exact byte string;\n`with space.txt` contains `ERROR4`, which is different literal text.\n\n~~~bash\nbash task.sh 'ERROR[42]' records/alpha.txt 'records/with space.txt' records/-dash.txt > first.bin 2> first.err\nsearch_status=$?\nprintf 'status=%s\\n' \"$search_status\"\ncat first.err\nwhile IFS= read -r -d '' file; do printf '%q\\n' \"$file\"; done < first.bin\n~~~\n\nIn the unfinished starter, the search and sort already run, but the final\nstatus interpretation is the named TODO and always returns 2. Complete the\nlast decision using both saved array entries. A grep status of 1 is a valid\nno-match outcome, while any nonzero sort status and a grep status greater\nthan 1 are failures. Keep the NUL separator, literal matching and input guards.\nCompare the reference after recording a working attempt.\n\n`PIPESTATUS` belongs to Bash and is replaced by the next foreground command,\neven a diagnostic print. The assignment immediately after the pipeline saves\nboth original entries. `set +e` prevents an ordinary no-match status from\nending the script before that assignment; `set -e` is restored afterward.\n`pipefail` alone supplies one aggregate status and cannot explain which tool\nfailed. Never add `|| true` and then report the search as successful.\n\n## Changed cases and a filename containing a newline\n\nKeep each run's output and diagnostic files separate. Use literal `ABSENT`\nagainst all three supplied files: expect an empty NUL stream and status 1 in\nthe completed implementation. Repeat the matching call with `records/alpha.txt`\ntwice: its identical path appears once after `sort -u`. Compare a literal\n`ERROR4` search against `ERROR[42]` and explain why a regular expression would\nanswer a different question.\n\nCreate the following disposable text file in the extracted role folder:\n\n~~~bash\nodd_file=$'records/two\\nlines.txt'\nprintf '00:09 INFO ERROR[42]\\n' > \"$odd_file\"\nbash task.sh 'ERROR[42]' \"$odd_file\" records/alpha.txt > newline.bin 2> newline.err\nsearch_status=$?\nprintf 'status=%s\\n' \"$search_status\"\nwhile IFS= read -r -d '' file; do printf '%q\\n' \"$file\"; done < newline.bin\nrm -- \"$odd_file\"\n~~~\n\nA line-based filename loop would split this one name. Retain the shell-quoted\nobservation and explain how the NUL boundary avoids that ambiguity.\n\nFinally pass `records/alpha.txt` followed by a nonexistent path. Expect grep's\nerror, status 2 from the completed script and possibly a partial filename list.\nAn unreadable ordinary file is another failure case when available in the\nowned lab; restore its original mode afterward. An empty list does not by\nitself distinguish no matches from a failed search. Reopen the saved workspace\nand retain the five completed notes tasks; a reference run does not complete\nthe evidence record.\n\nPrimary references: [GNU grep outcomes](https://www.gnu.org/s/grep/manual/html_node/Exit-Status.html),\n[GNU grep filename separators](https://www.gnu.org/s/grep/manual/grep.html),\n[GNU sort](https://www.gnu.org/s/coreutils/manual/html_node/sort-invocation.html),\nand [Bash pipelines](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Pipelines).\n",
	"LS-04-unit-1-shell-foundations-supplemental-3":
		"# Partial Input, Pipeline Statuses and Misleading Success\n\nThis optional extension follows the required log-summary project. It keeps a\nsmall two-stage pipeline visible: cat reads explicit text files, and awk counts\nexact level fields. The investigation is whether a printed summary proves that\nall input was read. Complete `pipeline-notes.md` using predictions and actual\nstdout, stderr and both stage statuses. The same pauses support independent\nreading and a paced walkthrough.\n\n## Equipment and contract\n\nUse an ordinary account in an owned Linux lab with Bash, cat and awk. Extract\none role with `count.awk` and the complete `records` folder. The website IDE\nsupports editing, saving and ZIP export; execute the pipeline in that external\nLinux lab. The program changes no inputs, permissions or system configuration.\n\n`bash task.sh FILE...` accepts one or more ordinary nonsymlink text-file paths.\nA standalone `-`, a directory, a symbolic link or a special file is rejected.\nA missing or unreadable path reaches the actual cat command and yields an input\nfailure. Quote each path; each operand is resolved from the caller's working\ndirectory. The supplied awk program is located beside `task.sh`, even when the\nscript is invoked through an absolute path from another directory.\n\nThe input format has a timestamp in field one, a level in field two and then a\nmessage. `count.awk` counts only exact ERROR and WARNING level fields. An INFO\nmessage containing ERROR contributes neither an error nor a warning. `ERROR[42]`\nis also a different level. On all three supplied files the complete result is\n`errors=2 warnings=2`. Empty successful input gives two zero counts.\n\nEvery run prints `producer=N consumer=N` to stderr after immediately saving\nBash's `PIPESTATUS`. In the reference, status 0 means both cat and awk exited 0;\nstatus 2 means invalid arguments or either stage failed. A printed summary\non status 2 is partial or unavailable, not a complete count. It is retained\nfor diagnosis, not hidden or replaced with invented zeroes.\n\n## Healthy prediction and the intentionally misleading starter\n\nFrom the selected role folder:\n\n~~~bash\nbash task.sh records/alpha.txt 'records/with space.txt' records/-dash.txt > healthy.txt 2> healthy.err\npipeline_status=$?\nprintf 'status=%s\\n' \"$pipeline_status\"\ncat healthy.txt\ncat healthy.err\n~~~\n\nThe unmodified starter succeeds here because its named TODO checks only awk.\nIts defect becomes visible when cat reads one valid file and then fails to read\nanother. Predict the partial counts before running this changed case:\n\n~~~bash\nbash task.sh records/alpha.txt records/absent.txt > partial.txt 2> partial.err\npipeline_status=$?\nprintf 'status=%s\\n' \"$pipeline_status\"\ncat partial.txt\ncat partial.err\n~~~\n\nWith `absent.txt` genuinely absent, cat exits nonzero after the valid first\nfile; awk still reaches its normal END block and prints `errors=1 warnings=1`.\nThe starter returns 0 because it ignores cat's saved status. Correct the named\ncondition to include both original array entries. The completed implementation\nreturns 2 for this run. Preserve the partial output and actual diagnostics, then\nexplain why neither a printed number nor a successful final stage proves that\nall input was available. Compare the reference after completing that repair.\n\n## Why the status capture is placed there\n\nBy default a pipeline's status is its last command's status. `set -o pipefail`\nchanges that aggregate to the rightmost nonzero status, but it does not identify\nall stage outcomes. `PIPESTATUS` records the separate stage results. The next\nforeground command replaces it, so the array assignment immediately follows\nthe pipeline. With `errexit` enabled a failing pipeline can stop the script\nbefore evidence is collected. The program uses `set +e` only around this\nobservation and restores `set -e` after saving the array. It then makes an\nexplicit final decision. Do not move a print, assignment to another variable,\nor `|| true` between the pipeline and the array capture.\n\n## Independent cases, consumer failure and restoration\n\nCreate an empty ordinary text file, run it as the sole input, and observe status\n0 with both zero counts after the repair. Copy `alpha.txt` to a filename with\nspaces and confirm that quoting keeps it one argument. Record what happens\nwhen a valid input follows a missing one; incomplete input remains a failure\nregardless of order.\n\nIn a disposable copy of the extracted role, preserve `count.awk` before making\nits syntax invalid. Append an unclosed BEGIN block, run one supplied input,\nthen restore the exact original awk file:\n\n~~~bash\ncp count.awk count.awk.original\nprintf '\\nBEGIN {\\n' >> count.awk\nbash task.sh records/alpha.txt > consumer.txt 2> consumer.err\npipeline_status=$?\nprintf 'status=%s\\n' \"$pipeline_status\"\ncat consumer.err\nmv -- count.awk.original count.awk\nbash task.sh records/alpha.txt > restored.txt 2> restored.err\nrestored_status=$?\nprintf 'restored=%s\\n' \"$restored_status\"\n~~~\n\nThe actual awk syntax error makes the consumer nonzero. Cat can either finish\nits small write before that failure or receive SIGPIPE; record the observed\nproducer status instead of assuming a particular ordering. The reference and\ncompleted starter return 2 either way. Restoring the original awk program and\nrerunning the same input demonstrates recovery. The valid one-file summary is\n`errors=1 warnings=1`.\n\nRetain five completed notes tasks, the changed-case outputs, the actual input\nfailure and consumer-failure diagnostics, and the restored result. Remove only\nthe disposable test files created in the selected role folder. Reopen the saved\nworkspace and explain the repair before comparing the reference. A successful\nreference run does not complete the learner's evidence record.\n\nPrimary references: [Bash pipelines](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Pipelines),\n[Bash PIPESTATUS](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Bash-Variables.html),\nand [GNU awk records and fields](https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/html_node/Fields.html).\n",
	"LS-05-unit-2-filesystem-hierarchy-and-directory-purpose-supplemental-2":
		'# Logical Paths, Physical Targets and Parent Traversal\n\nThis optional transfer follows the directory-purpose and static-site project.\nA path used by a service or shell can include a symbolic link. Its visible name,\nresolved target and parent traversal are separate observations. The project\nreports those paths; it does not serve files, install a release, change\npermissions or prove that a service is configured correctly. Complete\n`path-notes.md` with predictions and actual results. The same pauses support\nindependent study and a paced walkthrough.\n\n## Concepts and equipment\n\nUse an ordinary account in an owned Linux lab with Bash. The site IDE edits,\nsaves and exports the source; execute these commands in the external Linux\nterminal after extraction. The supplied program is read-only. The following\ncommands create a disposable directory/link example under one private parent.\nThey do not change `/srv`, `/etc`, `/var`, `/usr` or system configuration.\n\nA symbolic link stores another path. A relative link target is interpreted\nfrom the link\'s containing directory. Bash\'s logical directory mode retains\nthe traversed link spelling in PWD, while physical mode resolves the actual\ndirectory. In logical mode `..` removes the last logical path component; in\nphysical mode it moves to the resolved directory\'s parent. These can differ.\nNeither visible directory spelling nor an `ls` listing alone proves the target\nthat another program will open.\n\n## Create a real link and predict the four paths\n\nFrom the extracted role folder, retain the printed parent path in this shell:\n\n~~~bash\nlab_root=$(mktemp -d)\nprintf \'lab_root=%s\\n\' "$lab_root"\nmkdir -p "$lab_root/physical/site"\nprintf \'<h1>Owned example</h1>\\n\' > "$lab_root/physical/site/index.html"\nln -s physical/site "$lab_root/served"\nreadlink -- "$lab_root/served"\n~~~\n\nPredict these four values: logical directory, resolved physical directory,\nlogical parent and physical parent. For this arrangement the logical path ends\nin `/served`, the physical path ends in `/physical/site`, the logical parent is\nthe private parent, and the physical parent ends in `/physical`. The absolute\nprefix comes from this run; it is not supplied as an observation.\n\n~~~bash\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/served" > linked.paths 2> linked.err\ntrace_status=$?\nprintf \'status=%s\\n\' "$trace_status"\ncat linked.err\nwhile IFS= read -r -d \'\' field && IFS= read -r -d \'\' value; do\n    printf \'%s=%q\\n\' "$field" "$value"\ndone < linked.paths\n~~~\n\nThe output is four field/value pairs, each separated by NUL. NUL cannot occur\ninside a Unix path, while spaces and newlines can. The display loop quotes each\nvalue for inspection without splitting it. Keep the binary record alongside\nits decoded display. Do not use command substitution to store a NUL stream.\n\nIndependently compare Bash\'s own directory observations in a child shell:\n\n~~~bash\n(\n    cd -L -- "$lab_root/served"\n    pwd -L\n    pwd -P\n    (cd -L -- .. && pwd -L)\n    (cd -P -- .. && pwd -P)\n)\n~~~\n\nThe four printed lines correspond to the four recorded fields for this\nordinary path arrangement. The binary program output also preserves paths\ncontaining newlines, which plain line output cannot label unambiguously.\n\nThe starter deliberately computes the field named physical_parent using\nlogical traversal at its single TODO. Repair that flag so the field describes\nthe physical parent. Keep the other three observations, argument boundary and\nbinary stream unchanged. Compare the reference after recording a working\nattempt. Status 0 means all four observations completed; it does not mean the\nstarter\'s named decision is repaired or the worksheet is complete. Status 2\nmeans invalid input or an unsupported environment, and a failed directory\nchange can return 1. Discard a partial record as a complete result.\n\n## Compare direct access and another logical spelling\n\n~~~bash\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/physical/site" > direct.paths 2> direct.err\nln -s physical/site "$lab_root/another view"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/another view" > alternate.paths 2> alternate.err\n~~~\n\nThe two link spellings have different logical paths and the same physical\npath. Direct access has the same logical and physical directory. Record each\nrun\'s status and decode its record independently. Verify the document remains\nunchanged. A `cd` inside the program runs in a child shell and does not change\nthe caller\'s working directory.\n\nFor a changed case, create a failed target and then correct that exact link:\n\n~~~bash\nln -s missing-target "$lab_root/broken"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/broken" > broken.paths 2> broken.err\nbroken_status=$?\nprintf \'broken=%s\\n\' "$broken_status"\nrm -- "$lab_root/broken"\nln -s physical/site "$lab_root/broken"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/broken" > corrected.paths 2> corrected.err\ncorrected_status=$?\nprintf \'corrected=%s\\n\' "$corrected_status"\nexternal_root=$(mktemp -d)\nprintf \'external_root=%s\\n\' "$external_root"\nmkdir -- "$external_root/target"\nln -s "$external_root/target" "$lab_root/outside view"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/outside view" > outside.paths 2> outside.err\n~~~\n\nRecord how a name inside the first tree resolves outside that tree. The\nprogram reports the selected readable path and does not enforce containment.\nThis is a path-observation exercise, not a deployment or access-control check.\nNo arbitrary production path is needed for the experiment.\n\n## Retain evidence and clean up\n\nComplete the five notes tasks with the predicted and actual paths, the TODO\nrepair, direct/alternate comparisons, failed target and corrected target.\nVerify the source bytes, current identity and caller directory remain as\nrecorded. Remove the exact disposable directories after reviewing their\nprinted values; keep the saved workspace and result records separately.\n\n~~~bash\nprintf \'Review before removal: %q\\n\' "$lab_root" "$external_root"\nrm -r -- "$lab_root" "$external_root"\n~~~\n\nPrimary references: [Bash cd modes](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Bourne-Shell-Builtins.html)\nand [GNU symbolic links](https://www.gnu.org/s/coreutils/manual/html_node/ln-invocation.html).\n',
	"LS-06-unit-2-filesystem-hierarchy-and-directory-purpose-supplemental-3":
		'# Reported Size, Read Bytes and Live Kernel Files\n\nThis optional extension distinguishes a file\'s metadata, its mounted\nfilesystem and bytes returned by a real read. Linux kernel views such as\n`/proc/uptime` are generated information, not ordinary stored documents.\nComplete `filesystem-notes.md` with actual observations. The prediction,\ninspection and explanation pauses support independent study and a paced\nwalkthrough. This is separate from the core static-site deployment and from\nlater disk-capacity, archive and restore investigations.\n\n## Equipment and what the program measures\n\nUse an ordinary account in an owned Linux lab with Python 3.8 or later, Bash, GNU stat,\nGNU head and util-linux findmnt. Preserve `filesystem_observer.py` beside\n`task.sh` when extracting the role. The website IDE edits, saves and exports\nthese files; it does not provide the Linux kernel observations.\n\nThe program accepts one or more explicit paths. It resolves symbolic links,\nrejects directories, FIFOs, devices and a standalone `-`, and reads only\nregular files or readable regular-looking kernel pseudo-files. It never\nwrites to the selected inputs, changes mount configuration or invokes sudo.\nEach path is inspected by actual `stat`, `findmnt` and `head` commands, with\nseparate arguments, bounded subprocess time and retained statuses/output.\n\n`stat` reports metadata size, device and inode. `findmnt --target` identifies\nthe path\'s actual mounted filesystem; the program explicitly requests JSON\nTARGET, FSTYPE and SOURCE columns. A directory name is not enough to identify\nthat filesystem. `head` reads at most 4097 bytes. The report retains a maximum\n4096-byte base64 capture plus its SHA-256, records the actual bytes returned,\nand marks truncated captures. A large file\'s size can exceed that bounded\nread without being a kernel view. Metadata and content are observed by\nseparate commands, so a changing input is not one atomic snapshot.\n\n## Predict ordinary, empty and kernel-backed observations\n\nCreate two ordinary inputs under a private disposable directory. Keep the\nprinted parent path for this shell:\n\n~~~bash\nlab_root=$(mktemp -d)\nprintf \'lab_root=%s\\n\' "$lab_root"\nprintf \'alpha\\n\' > "$lab_root/ordinary.txt"\n: > "$lab_root/empty.txt"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/ordinary.txt" "$lab_root/empty.txt" /proc/uptime > first.json 2> first.err\nobserver_status=$?\nprintf \'status=%s\\n\' "$observer_status"\ncat first.err\ncat first.json\n~~~\n\nPredict size 6 and six read bytes for the supplied ordinary file, and zero\nsize/zero read bytes for the supplied empty file. Read `/proc/uptime` rather\nthan supplying sample numbers. On the target Linux environment it reports\nmetadata size 0 while returning a nonempty live record with two uptime/idle\ntime quantities. Identify its actual filesystem type from findmnt rather than\nfrom its size alone. Record the observed values and explain why reported size\n0 cannot establish that this generated file is empty.\n\nThe starter\'s named content_is_empty TODO uses only reported metadata size.\nRepair it using the count from the successful bounded read. The supplied\nreference gives a method, not prefilled observations. Status 0 means every\nrequested observation completed; it does not establish worksheet completion\nor repair of the starter\'s decision. A native command failure returns 1,\ninvalid/unreadable path selection or unsupported environment can return 2,\nand an interruption returns 130. On failure stdout contains no successful\ncomplete report; stderr retains the error, actual tool records and any earlier\ncompleted observations. Do not treat a partly completed error record as the\nnormal all-path result.\n\n## Independently compare actual tools and decode a capture\n\n~~~bash\nstat -L --printf=\'reported bytes=%s device=%d inode=%i\\n\' -- /proc/uptime\nfindmnt --json --target /proc/uptime --output TARGET,FSTYPE,SOURCE\nhead --bytes=4097 -- /proc/uptime > independent-uptime.txt\nread_status=$?\nprintf \'read_status=%s\\n\' "$read_status"\nwc -c < independent-uptime.txt\npython3 - <<\'CHECK\'\nimport base64, json\nwith open(\'first.json\', encoding=\'utf-8\') as source:\n    result = json.load(source)\nfor entry in result[\'observations\']:\n    print(entry[\'inputPath\'], repr(base64.b64decode(entry[\'capturedBase64\'])))\nCHECK\n~~~\n\nIndependent uptime reads occur at different times. Compare meaning, the\nnonempty read and filesystem context; do not require identical live numbers.\nThe bytes stored inside one report and their stored checksum do remain exact\nand can be checked independently. The program does not identify a particular\nperson\'s usage or infer persistent storage from a live kernel record.\n\n## Changed cases, failure and recovery\n\nUse a long ordinary input and a quoted path, then retain a later live read:\n\n~~~bash\npython3 -c "print(\'x\' * 5000, end=\'\')" > "$lab_root/long.txt"\ncp -- "$lab_root/ordinary.txt" "$lab_root/with space.txt"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/long.txt" "$lab_root/with space.txt" /proc/uptime > changed.json 2> changed.err\nchanged_status=$?\nprintf \'changed=%s\\n\' "$changed_status"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/ordinary.txt" "$lab_root/absent.txt" > failed.json 2> failed.err\nfailed_status=$?\nprintf \'failed=%s\\n\' "$failed_status"\ncat failed.err\n~~~\n\nThe long input has metadata size 5000, returns 4097 bytes and retains 4096;\ntruncated is true. Compare these fields before interpreting a mismatch.\nPreserve the missing-path error record and verify no complete stdout result\nwas claimed. To observe an actual native read denial on a disposable copy:\n\n~~~bash\ncp -- "$lab_root/ordinary.txt" "$lab_root/private.txt"\nsaved_mode=$(stat -c \'%a\' -- "$lab_root/private.txt")\nchmod 000 -- "$lab_root/private.txt"\nbash task.sh "$lab_root/private.txt" > denied.json 2> denied.err\ndenied_status=$?\nprintf \'denied=%s\\n\' "$denied_status"\nchmod "$saved_mode" -- "$lab_root/private.txt"\ncat denied.err\n~~~\n\nCorrect the missing path and repeat the same comparison to verify recovery.\nRetain five completed notes tasks, actual tool outputs, the changed input and\nfailed/restored records. Reopen the saved workspace before comparing the\nreference. Remove only the exact disposable input directory after reviewing\nits printed value; retain result records separately.\n\n~~~bash\nprintf \'Review before removal: %q\\n\' "$lab_root"\nrm -r -- "$lab_root"\n~~~\n\nPrimary references: [GNU stat](https://www.gnu.org/s/coreutils/manual/html_node/stat-invocation.html),\n[GNU head](https://www.gnu.org/s/coreutils/manual/html_node/head-invocation.html),\n[findmnt](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/findmnt.8.html), and\n[Linux procfs](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc.5.html).\n'
};

